window.harnessChunks["rows-1968f3b45afd248c.js"]=["<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-24T23:33:32+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/phodal/routa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Routa</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-24\">2026-10-24</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/phodal/routa/tree/9dc7b7be8fa92b760f6ebf0ac7f8b44db1b4dc34\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 9dc7b7be8fa92b760f6ebf0ac7f8b44db1b4dc34\">9dc7b7b &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Carry out a bounded software implementation or verification task using a model-backed CRAFTER/GATE or ordinary supported agent session. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unknown repository layout, changing test outcomes, error messages, incomplete acceptance evidence and tool-execution results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The installed provider-backed role reads the task and repository observations and chooses which edits, tests, queries or further actions to perform; the task/result feedback can change the next decision. - Decision owner: The composed autonomous CRAFTER/GATE or session agent, not the ACP transport, fixed board scheduler or person selecting a provider. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Bundled specialist role prompts, ACP process spawning, workspace/scoped task state, tools, traces, test commands, sandboxes and status updates. - Closure path: Task → agent action and observed code/test result → subsequent agent decision → report/verdict and durable task state. - Boundary reachability: Standard Team/Kanban configurations explicitly ship the roles, prompt contracts and ACP spawn path, so user integration code is not needed beyond installing/configuring a supported provider. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Inference and selected code actions are external-provider computation, but Routa&#39;s shipped role adapters purposefully compose that actor into an operating task loop. Bare Kanban automation is not independently credited as an agent. - Evidence: CRAFTER contract; delegation and execution; session guide. - Basis: Supported composition with a separate decision-making agent actor and action/observation loop. - Confidence: High for shipped wiring; functional quality of model output not benchmarked. - Caveats: No live model/provider run was conducted for this index assessment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Regulate interference between concurrently operating CRAFTER cells on tasks in one workspace. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two implementors choosing overlapping files or incompatible changes, duplicate work, and contention for the board&#39;s finite concurrent execution slots. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Bundled model-backed coordinator/implementors are instructed to inspect other active agents and their scope, message the affected agent or parent and adjust assignments/actions; the board&#39;s deterministic queue separately enforces configured capacity. - Decision owner: In the shipped Team mode, coordinator and implementor agents make the discretionary interference judgments; the fixed queue owns neither assignment priorities nor the model&#39;s conflict-resolution decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: listagents, readagentconversation, sendmessagetoagent, scoped tasks, working directories, waitMode=afterall and concurrency queue. - Closure path: Concurrent S1 actor status/scope → model-backed conflict check/communication → changed file choice, work timing or escalation → resumed individual S1 behavior. Queue events also prevent a second S1 starting before capacity releases. - Boundary reachability: The bundled CRAFTER prompt explicitly requires an active-agent conflict preflight and parent notification, with corresponding first-party tools and ACP messaging in the supported setup. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Only the shipped agent&#39;s discretionary conflict response supports A; an event bus, message API, queue or hard semaphore by itself would not. - Distinct S1 units: At least two separately spawned CRAFTER agents undertaking different task blocks, potentially sharing a repository/workspace. - Inter-S1 disturbance: File/feature overlap, incompatible changes and oversubscribed board slots from parallel task execution. - Attenuating coordination relation: Before editing, a CRAFTER checks other agents and sends a coordination/escalation message; the ROUTA model can divide tasks into isolated scopes or regroup work; board concurrency imposes a deterministic upper bound. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: The agent changes its scope, asks parent to reassign, or waits; the queue releases another task on predecessor completion. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The bundled preflight is explicitly tied to avoiding concurrent file conflicts, with actionable cross-agent status and returned messages. The queue is a second anti-contention witness, not the sole justification. - Evidence: conflict preflight in bundled CRAFTER role, agent tools, bounded concurrency and lifecycle. - Basis: Agent-discretionary anti-overlap instruction and first-party cross-agent feedback in the shipped composed Team mode. - Confidence: Medium; active-agent conflict checks are prompt-level rather than enforced hard locks. - Caveats: No measured rate of conflict avoidance; deterministic queue tests establish queue behavior, not model compliance with prompts.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Maintain an actionable current view of implementation commitments and change which agents/tasks/waves are active in response to reported progress and failure. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Incomplete waves, failing verifications, mismatched task scopes, agent stalls, failed provider sessions and changing prioritization of current delivery work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The model-backed ROUTA coordinator decides decomposition, who gets a task, the next wave, whether to request GATE verification, whether failed work needs another CRAFTER and when to finalize a spec. - Decision owner: The deployed ROUTA coordinator model/agent. The human&#39;s required initial plan approval constrains the mandate but does not itself execute the subsequent agent-owned current-control wave and repair decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Workspace-wide agent/task listing, spec notes, @@@task parser, task statuses, child delegation, completion-group collation, wake-parent messages, review verdicts and watchdog/queue limits. - Closure path: Whole-workspace tasks/agents and child reports → coordinator assessment of current state → selected agent assignments or repair wave → new child sessions → completion signals returned to coordinator. - Boundary reachability: The standard bundled coordinator prompt mandates the entire wave/verify/repair workflow, and RoutaOrchestrator.wakeParent prompts the actual coordinator session with child results, re-entering agent discretion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The decisive present-work portfolio choice is performed by an agent at the parent-of-S1 scope. A static column sequence, depth limit or queue max would only enforce that choice, not own it. - Whole-system current view: Spec note and scoped task list, listagents and agent conversations, completed/active statuses, child results and report/verification summaries for one workspace. - Current-control decision scope: Decomposition, assignments, batches/waves, interventions after failed verification and integration or completion of current commitments; not provider-wide global budgets or OSS contributor governance. - Evidence: ROUTA control contract, spawning and task assignment, feedback wakes coordinator. - Basis: Agent-owned discretionary current control in packaged coordinator mode; no additional (P) mode claimed from simple approval or dashboard intervention. - Confidence: Medium-high for supported path, without evidence of deployed model reliability. - Caveats: Human approves the initial specification; the path assessed is subsequent bounded agent control, not independent organizational charter changes.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Independently check CRAFTER implementation and completion claims against the original acceptance criteria, with verdicts returned into current coordination. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Self-serving or mistaken implementation success reports, untested code, missing requirements, scope creep and incomplete evidence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A separately spawned, model-backed GATE role examines the specification, implementation changes and tests and chooses APPROVED/NOT APPROVED/BLOCKED; it need not accept the CRAFTER&#39;s own report. - Decision owner: A distinct verifier agent session, not the acting CRAFTER or a deterministic policy regex. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Read-only verification role contract, access to task/spec and agent conversations, test/diff evidence, verification verdict storage and review-column routing. - Closure path: Acting agent claims completion → separate verifier obtains spec, diff/tests and reports → verifier produces verdict → ROUTA receives report and/or board returns to Dev/Blocked or advances to Done. - Boundary reachability: The standard ROUTA specialist workflow explicitly launches a separate GATE agent after CRAFTER completion; the GATE role and review-verdict feedback path are bundled, not custom add-ons. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Verifier performs its own evidence judgment; lane transitions and deterministic fitness checks carry/enforce its output but do not replace it. Independence is role/session separation, not a guarantee of different provider or institutional independence. - Claim being audited: CRAFTER&#39;s statement that an implementation satisfies the task and tests. - Ordinary reporting path: CRAFTER reports files changed, verification performed and success through reporttoparent/task state. - Complementary access path: GATE separately reads the original spec, developer conversation, diffs, commits and test evidence through its own session/tools. - Independence boundary: Separate verifier specialist, prompt and ACP session, barred by its role from editing/rewriting the requirements; they may still share provider vendor and workspace access. - Who acts on findings: ROUTA re-delegates if deficiencies are reported; review-lane convergence uses NOTAPPROVED→Dev, BLOCKED→Blocked and APPROVED→Done. - Evidence: GATE verifier and evidence contract, coordinator GATE delegation, verdict routing. - Basis: A second operational actor has a different evidence route, own judgment and a return path to controlling subsequent work. - Confidence: Medium-high on wiring, not on accuracy of model review. - Caveats: Shared model/provider and same operator are potential correlated-failure channels; no LLM efficacy or blind-review experiments performed.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No material first-party, closed prospective external-environment sensing and future capability-adaptation loop demonstrated at this local delivery-organization recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New programming practices, provider capabilities, framework changes or future market requirements could challenge Routa, but responding within today&#39;s task is not itself prospective strategy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: None shown for future organizational capability adaptation; the agent coordinator reprioritizes current task waves, while operators/maintainers choose new products, providers or policy/configuration. - Decision owner: No S4-specific first-party autonomous or parent-mode actor evidenced at the assessed recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Trace history, run-outcome persistence, learned playbooks, context preload, configurable specialists, repo fitness checks, adapter selection and future-looking docs. - Closure path: Retrospective playbook hints may improve a later similar task, but the reviewed shipped paths do not close outside-and-then scanning → prospective option selection → adopted organizational capability change. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Local reuse and incident recovery are present-work learning; future externally oriented system adaptation remains unestablished. - Evidence: trace/run memory in coordinator pipeline, run-outcome and playbook sync, architecture. - Basis: Bounded absence of future/external intelligence closure in standard operational mode. - Confidence: Medium. - Caveats: Specialized outside-facing workflows could be configured by users but are not credited absent a shipped mode and ownership path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party route is shown for deciding ultimate organization identity or policy conflicts and returning such decisions into the delivery organization&#39;s ongoing behavior. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Mandate, security and permission disputes might threaten autonomy, but per-tool permissions and plan approval are narrower than an ultimate-policy dispute. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Not established for identity-level governance at the selected workspace organization; users approve an initial plan, coordinators resolve ordinary tool permission requests, and config/operators set provider/sandbox rules. - Decision owner: Operator for startup approval and ordinary restrictions; ROUTA for delegated tool permissions; neither mechanism is an evidenced S5 agent or parent policy closure. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Initial plan approval, coordinator permission request/response, sandbox constraint mutation, agent-role instructions and local runtime policy. - Closure path: Permission response can authorize or deny one execution, but there is no identity/ultimate-policy escalation, authoritative resolution and standing return-to-operation path for S5 at this recursion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Supervising execution permissions is not equivalent to governing the organization&#39;s identity or highest-order constraints. - Evidence: approval before delegation, permission request and coordinator response. - Basis: Bounded absence of S5-specific agent or parent governance closure. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A company operating Routa might establish its own legitimate S5 externally; it is not supplied by this local first-party harness mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/be35ca0f36f0861462993a7631c4ccc3ec0f9b6b/assessments/ro/routa.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit be35ca0f36f0861462993a7631c4ccc3ec0f9b6b\">be35ca0 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-20T20:26:02Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Untrivial-ai/agent-orchestrator\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Agent Orchestrator</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-20\">2026-09-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Untrivial-ai/agent-orchestrator/tree/1e4a394b20c470d281b2a7f6f63fd47c30af5019\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 1e4a394b20c470d281b2a7f6f63fd47c30af5019\">1e4a394 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously implement and verify one bounded software-engineering task in an AO-managed worker workspace and produce task/branch/PR outcomes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repository structure, implementation ambiguity, tool/test observations, task-specific failures, CI/review follow-up and direct messages relevant to the assigned task. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose implementation/tool actions and iterate on the assigned task within AO&#39;s project/workspace/publishing constraints. - Decision owner: the model-driven coding-agent worker session launched and managed by AO. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AO session manager, selected harness adapter, isolated worker worktree/branch, task prompt/project rules, session persistence, messaging, PR association and lifecycle APIs. - Closure path: assigned task/issue → AO worker session → model-driven code/tool/test actions → repository/tool/CI/review observations → further worker action → commits/PR/task result. - Boundary reachability: AO&#39;s standard spawn/delegation surfaces directly create KindWorker sessions under the first-party worker role and project/worktree lifecycle; downstream code does not have to construct a new organizational loop. External coding-agent/model software supplies inference/execution substrate but does not supply the AO project organization credited above it. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: implementation and local task choices are made by the running worker agent from current evidence rather than by the deterministic AO daemon or a human preauthoring each action. - Evidence: README.md, backend/internal/sessionmanager/prompt.go, backend/internal/service/session/delegation.go. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: external coding-agent harnesses retain their own internal loop semantics; this assessment credits only the supported AO-managed operational path and does not import those harnesses&#39; metasystem functions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate overlapping worker ownership and concurrent repository-write interference among distinct coding-worker S1 units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: duplicate workers acting on the same current task/PR and concurrent workers colliding through shared mutable repository state. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide whether new work needs a new worker versus an existing worker redirect/message, maintain visible ownership, and keep concurrent worker writes isolated. - Decision owner: the persistent project orchestrator for ownership/duplicate-work attenuation; AO&#39;s deterministic worktree/session machinery enforces workspace isolation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: live worker/ownership state, ao status, worker/session lookup, ao spawn, ao send, worker redirect/kill surfaces, per-worker branches/worktrees and project-scoped session state. - Closure path: current worker ownership + requested/new work → orchestrator inspects current state → spawn existing/new or redirect/message decision → AO session/worktree control → subsequent worker assignment and writes reflect that coordination decision. - Boundary reachability: the first-party orchestrator system prompt mandates ownership inspection and duplicate avoidance and exposes the required AO commands in the standard project-orchestrator session; isolated worktrees are also standard AO worker behavior. No downstream coordinator policy has to be authored to obtain these relations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: worktree allocation alone would be deterministic support, but the positive A claim rests on the model orchestrator&#39;s explicit current ownership check and discretionary new-worker-versus-redirect decision over distinct live workers. - Evidence: README.md, backend/internal/sessionmanager/prompt.go. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic worker messaging and task delegation are not the basis. The credited S2 witness is specifically duplicate/overlap attenuation plus workspace collision isolation among concurrently viable workers. - Distinct S1 units: concurrently active AO coding-worker sessions, each responsible for a bounded task or PR. - Inter-S1 disturbance: two workers can duplicate ownership of the same task/PR or interfere through concurrent writes against one shared checkout/branch state. - Attenuating coordination relation: the orchestrator must inspect ownership/current sessions before spawn and reuse/redirect/message a suitable active worker instead of duplicating work; AO additionally gives Git-backed workers isolated worktrees/branches. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the orchestrator&#39;s spawn/redirect/message choice changes which worker owns the work and what that worker does next, while AO&#39;s chosen workspace constrains subsequent writes. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the evidence names concrete cross-worker disturbances—duplicate active ownership and mutable-workspace collision—and first-party relations that suppress those disturbances before subsequent worker action.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: regulate the project-wide current portfolio of worker commitments, priorities, ownership, blockers and PR/CI/review interventions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing project priorities, concurrent worker capacity, duplicate/obsolete commitments, stuck workers, CI failures, requested review changes, task/PR ownership and sequencing across the whole current project. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose or revise which current work should exist, which worker owns it, whether to spawn/redirect/message/kill a worker and what current issue/CI/review feedback should be routed into that work. - Decision owner: base A mode — the persistent model-driven project orchestrator; parent P mode — the human operator using AO&#39;s project-wide current view and task/session feedback/control surfaces. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: project/orchestrator state, ao status, project Kanban/current worker view, session list/get, spawn/send/kill APIs, PR/CI/review state and persistent project conversation/history. - Closure path: whole-project live state → orchestrator or parent current-control judgment → spawn/redirect/send/kill/task-feedback action → AO updates/contacts the relevant worker → subsequent project commitments/current operation change. - Boundary reachability: AO creates the persistent project orchestrator as a supported first-party session with the current-control role and commands already injected. The desktop/project/Kanban surfaces expose the same current project state and worker controls to the human parent without requiring a downstream control plane. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the base mode is more than deterministic scheduling: the model orchestrator is explicitly told to reason over ownership, priorities, blockers, CI/reviews and select interventions. The parent mode is separately closed because the human sees the same whole-project state and can return actionable current decisions through AO. - Evidence: README.md, backend/internal/sessionmanager/prompt.go, backend/internal/httpd/controllers/sessions.go. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: daemon/session lifecycle mechanics, concurrency enforcement and status persistence support S3 but are not independently treated as autonomous current-control owners. - Whole-system current view: project orchestrator/desktop state combines active workers, ownership, tasks/PRs, CI, reviews and blockers across the project rather than one worker only. - Current-control decision scope: assign or revise worker commitments and priorities, avoid duplicate active work, redirect/message stuck or existing workers, spawn additional workers and terminate sessions where appropriate.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge the correctness/readiness of worker PR output through a separate read-only reviewer and return adverse findings into the responsible worker. - Disturbance / variety regulated: worker implementation defects, missing error handling/security/test coverage/convention violations and other discrepancies that ordinary worker self-report or CI may not reveal. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect the worker&#39;s PR diff/base evidence under the reviewer role and decide approved versus changesrequested with concrete findings. - Decision owner: a separately launched model-driven AO reviewer session under the configured reviewer harness. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: review engine/run store, stable reviewer handle, reviewer-specific system/task prompts, PR/head selection, auto-review coordinator, review result persistence, current-head delivery filter and lifecycle sendOnce nudge. - Closure path: eligible idle worker + current PR head → separate AO reviewer reads diff/evidence → reviewer submits verdict/findings → service keeps current-head changesrequested results → lifecycle ApplyReviewBatch sends findings to worker → worker receives corrective work order and subsequent implementation changes can follow. - Boundary reachability: AO ships the reviewer engine, reviewer adapters/runtime launcher, auto-review coordinator, reviewer role and lifecycle delivery. Enabling the first-party auto-review mode on a session/project is sufficient to close the path; no external reviewer agent organization or custom corrective bridge is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the audit judgment is made by the independent reviewer model session, not by deterministic CI parsing or the worker being audited; AO then closes the returned finding into the worker automatically. - Evidence: backend/internal/review/review.go, backend/internal/review/launcher.go, backend/internal/review/prompt.go, backend/internal/autoreview/coordinator.go, backend/internal/service/review/review.go, backend/internal/lifecycle/reactions.go. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: ordinary external GitHub review and CI observations are not credited as AO-owned S3. The positive result rests on AO&#39;s own separate reviewer subsystem and the supported auto-review/delivery closure. - Claim being audited: the worker&#39;s current PR/implementation is correct and ready enough to proceed without reviewer-requested changes. - Ordinary reporting path: worker implementation, tests/commits/PR output, session status and normal PR/CI state observed by AO. - Complementary access path: a separate reviewer process reads the worker worktree and PR diff against its base under reviewer-only instructions and submits an independent review verdict/body. - Independence boundary: reviewer has a distinct runtime identity and prompt role and is explicitly forbidden to edit, stage, commit, push, switch branches or run project programs/tests/builds/installers/generators; it challenges evidence rather than producing the implementation. - Who acts on findings: AO lifecycle delivery routes current-head changesrequested findings back to the responsible worker, which performs the corrective implementation; the project orchestrator/human can additionally observe the review state.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party prospective environment-sensing and capability-adaptation loop established at this project recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: persistent project conversation/history, repository context, issue/PR/CI/review inputs and planning prompts provide current work context but do not by themselves own future capability adaptation. - Closure path: no qualifying sensing → adaptation-option selection → return into reusable future capability path established. - Boundary reachability: not applicable — no positive S4 ownership path is credited. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the project orchestrator can plan future project work, but planning current/future deliverables is not evidence that AO adapts the organization&#39;s reusable capability from prospective environmental intelligence. - Evidence: README.md, backend/internal/sessionmanager/prompt.go. - Basis: explicit absence after boundary review. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: model agents may use web/tools in individual tasks, but that is operational task work unless a separate prospective adaptation closure is shown.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity / ultimate-policy closure established at the declared project recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as S5. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: none established for a qualifying S5 loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: project rules, worker/orchestrator standing prompts, publishing scope, harness/model selection, permission modes and explicit user restrictions constrain action but are authored/configured operating policy rather than a demonstrated identity/ultimate-policy decision loop. - Closure path: no qualifying identity/policy issue → ultimate authority decision → returned governance of subsequent operation loop established. - Boundary reachability: not applicable — no positive S5 ownership path is credited. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the orchestrator follows project/user policy and may reason about product direction, but evidence does not show it owns ultimate organizational identity or policy authority; human configuration/requests likewise do not by themselves establish a complete parent S5 loop. - Evidence: backend/internal/sessionmanager/prompt.go, README.md. - Basis: explicit absence after boundary review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: parent-governed S3 does not imply parent-governed S5; current-control authority and ultimate identity/policy authority are separate functions.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/5fdd7d59ec60b4c4b16a73b981652d52e017244d/assessments/agent-orchestrator.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 5fdd7d59ec60b4c4b16a73b981652d52e017244d\">5fdd7d5 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-25T15:50:52+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Open-Makers/ai-coding-agents-orchestrator\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AI Coding Agents Orchestrator</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-25\">2026-10-25</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Open-Makers/ai-coding-agents-orchestrator/tree/1c1185c6f323bf3475c3847e38245276852ebc5e\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 1c1185c6f323bf3475c3847e38245276852ebc5e\">1c1185c &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Interpret a coding task, produce/test/edit source files and deliver model-mediated QA/implementation results within the active project. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User goals, repository structure, compiler/test diagnostics, code changes, alternative implementation paths and failures under active work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model-backed Coder decides concrete implementation/fix content from specifications and current source/test observations; QA chooses test content; PM develops an actionable decomposition. Actual code/test outputs feed later work. - Decision owner: The first-party configured and invoked Go model-backed agents and their separate runner.LLMRunners, assembled by standard main and TaskRunner. External model inference/vendor process logic is a dependency, not assigned independent organizational governance. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Local workspace/artifact stores, test executor, context source collection, role-specific prompts, optional beads dependency claims, budgets, bounded retries, event bus, source writes and human task gates. - Closure path: User task → PM negotiation/approved spec → model-produced sub-tasks → QA-generated tests / Coder source work and build results → model-driven fixes → audited deliverable/status. - Boundary reachability: cmd/orchestrator/main.go instantiates all roles; TaskRunner.run directly calls executeBeads and implementBead, which route to real model-backed role agents and workspace mutation; no custom downstream operator needs to implement the actor loop for normal use. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Without model role decisions the program can copy artifacts, enforce test bounds and sequence phases but cannot interpret arbitrary coding goals or choose implementation actions. This is first-party assembled autonomous S1, not evidence of first-party model weights. - Evidence: standard role setup; subtask execution and actual code fixes; first-party role-specific QA inference. - Basis: Standard first-party model/role/workspace feedback paths; no live model-effectiveness test. - Confidence: High for program structure, no empirical success claim. - Caveats: Model/provider is external; the unsupported absence of a provider prevents a live session but not a shipped standard model-backed mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Resolve a concrete inter-S1 incompatibility between QA-authored tests and Coder-authored source, preventing unproductive coder-fix versus test-requirement oscillation in the active project. - Disturbance / variety regulated: The coder may repeatedly fail QA-generated tests either because code is wrong or because test assumptions are incorrect; retrying only the coder can cause persistent interference between independent work products. - Distinct S1 units: QAAgent creates actual test files, and CoderAgent produces implementation/fixes; both are separately instantiated role actors and output into the same project. - Inter-S1 disturbance: globalBuildFix observes repeated build/test failure and escalates to QA to discriminate incorrect tests from deficient code. The origin is concrete incompatible code/test artifacts, not mere parallel invocation. - Attenuating coordination relation: QAAgent.VerifyTests reopens production-source/test files via safefile.ReadFile, judges test correctness, and either returns TestsOK (force coder to retry its side) or emits updated test files (repair the QA side). The harness merges changed paths and resumes coder fixes. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: UpdatedFiles are written to the worktree and merged into the files passed to the next Coder fix; a TestsOK response records that QA&#39;s tests should stand and Coder work must continue. Retry/QA-verification caps prevent endless cycling. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A separate model-backed QA role owns the distinction between valid and faulty tests, specifically at the cross-unit conflict boundary. Deterministic counters/escalation trigger and file merge enforce that judgment. - Decision owner: Autonomous first-party QAAgent configured by standard main; no external human must personally judge each test-versus-code conflict in the normal path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: defaultMaxCoderAttempts, maxQAVerifyRounds, test reports, separate QA role prompt, source file reader and updated-file parser/writer. - Closure path: QA tests + coder implementation → actual failed build/test loop → QA model reads both sides and chooses retain/fix tests → changed files / judgment return to coder → renewed build/test execution. - Boundary reachability: Standard globalBuildFix is invoked after the implementation of all sub-tasks; it calls installed QA agent&#39;s VerifyTests after bounded failed attempts. This is not a generic queue or hypothetical coordination extension. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The logic explicitly recognizes a disagreement between separate S1-produced artifacts, invokes a function-specific adjudication to identify which side is wrong and feeds the outcome back into their subsequent work. The fixed number of attempts is mere enforcement, not the S2 ownership proof. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Remove the QA model judgment and only retries/fail counts remain; the code no longer decides whether to rewrite tests or require implementation correction. This narrow S2 right is model-owned (A), not inferred from the orchestrator name. - Evidence: QA TDD, Coder and escalation; independent QA source and test verdict. - Basis: Concrete first-party inter-S1 artifact conflict + autonomous resolution + operational return. - Confidence: Medium-high for installed path; no claim QA&#39;s judgment is factually correct. - Caveats: QA authored the tests it evaluates, so this narrow coordination decision is not itself the independent S3 audit witness. Other cross-agent resource contention and infinite loop avoidance are not inferred from it.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Govern the currently approved project implementation as a whole by judging multi-reviewer feedback, deciding actionable commitments and directing new corrective sub-tasks to the active work cells. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Aggregate QA, UX and Security defects threaten the deliverable after ordinary coder completion; individual workers cannot decide which whole-project issues are blocking versus deferrable. - Whole-system current view: TaskRunner retains approved spec, task plan/sub-task beads, implementation status, current source files and QA/UX/Security review feedback. The PM arbitration call receives all three reviewer channels in one input and can distinguish current blockers from nice-to-haves. - Current-control decision scope: PM model decides pass versus real fixes, NiceToHave deferral and new remediation SubTasks/review scope; TaskRunner replaces the next iteration&#39;s active tasks with these sub-tasks, creates new beads and reruns implement+review. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A model-backed PM ArbitrateAll deliberates the current global review state and selects the next project-wide commitments; the Go loop limits iteration count and applies task allocations but does not choose which findings warrant fixes. - Decision owner: First-party configured autonomous PM role, invoked in normal green/brown/fix implementation+review pipelines. Human approves initial scope/spec/plan but does not own each automated PM remediation choice in that mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Shared task/artifact store, beads and dependency tracking, QA/UX/security reports, fix-round counter, maxArbitrateIterations/maxFixIterations, event/status and branch/file outcome recording. - Closure path: Complete work across sub-tasks → code-reality review by QA/UX/Security → PM verdict → fresh sub-tasks/beads → Coder/QA perform remediations → new whole-project review until pass/cap. - Boundary reachability: implementAndReview is called by the shipped run for standard green/brown/fix pipelines. Its pm.ArbitrateAll result is directly consumed to rewrite the task list for the next execution loop. Reduced R&amp;D mode is explicitly excluded from this claim. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: If PM is removed, TaskRunner takes a permissive &quot;accepting review as pass&quot; fallback; no autonomous whole-project prioritization remains. Model PM owns the substantive current-control judgment, not the deterministic iterator. - Evidence: run modes and user gates; PM remediation feedback loop; autonomous PM arbitration call/parser. - Basis: Positive installed current-plan/review → discretionary new commitments → execution return. - Confidence: Medium-high for structural model-owned intervention, no runtime effectiveness assertion. - Caveats: Whole-system is the one active coding-project/task recursion, not all projects of the OSS maintainer organization. Human approval of task scope is an operational gate, not a separate evidenced S3 parent-owned mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Supply complementary raw-code reality to challenge normal Coder-produced outputs and return actionable independent defects to the current PM control loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Coder might report completion or pass its internal checks while source contains correctness, security or UX defects that routine coder reporting does not reveal. - Claim being audited: Coder&#39;s claim that implemented project files and a passing ordinary build/test phase are sufficiently correct and safe for acceptance. - Ordinary reporting path: Coder returns implementation files/build outcome through its first-party role and normal TaskRunner status; the project may otherwise be marked implemented after a build loop. - Complementary access path: Independently instantiated Security/QA/UX reviewer roles receive actual filesystem-read project source context assembled by buildCompactSourceContext, plus test-result artifacts. This is different access from accepting the Coder&#39;s completion text. Source context is bounded/truncated; if no source is available, a fallback to raw Coder output weakens independence and is not the positive basis. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Dedicated model-backed Security and QA review agents inspect the code and judge Approved versus concrete MustFix; Security does not implement the Coder change in this path. QA may also generate tests elsewhere, but Security&#39;s separate source-reading role anchors the independent code-audit claim. - Decision owner: Separate first-party model-backed reviewer role(s), with dedicated prompts and source read permissions; audit judgments remain autonomous while Go transport and parsing are deterministic. - Closure path: Actual files → independent role review → reviewer MustFix/unparsed concern → qualityReview channels → PM ArbitrateAll → accepted blocking issues become new remediation sub-tasks → coder changes real files and subsequent reviewers recheck. - Who acts on findings: The separately modeled PM agent decides which reviewer defects become blocking new sub-tasks, while Coder implements fixes and reviewers subsequently revisit the changed sources. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: safefile.ReadFile, import/caller context expansion, source/test artifacts, separate role agents and logs, fixed iteration caps and parse-to-feedback mapping. - Independence boundary: Security is created separately from Coder and reads code anew from workspace paths. This is independent with respect to ordinary Coder-produced success claims, though it shares project/operator configuration and model provider infrastructure. It is not a statistically independent outcome study. - Boundary reachability: Standard main.go creates Security and QA roles independently from Coder; normal qualityReview after implementation invokes those agents with actual project file paths and routes their returned results to PM arbitration. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Remove separate reviewer cognition and only source ingestion and deterministic status remain. The auditor&#39;s qualitative distinction among must-fix and acceptable code is model-owned, while PM separately owns corrective prioritization, preserving roles. - Evidence: default separate agents; independent source reader; Security raw source versus coder-output fallback; audit-to-PM corrective return. - Basis: Source-first alternative evidence + dedicated autonomous independent reviewer + distinct S3 return. - Confidence: Medium. Actual raw-source availability and result correctness were not measured; independence is structural and claim-relative. - Caveats: QA test-author reviewing its own tests is not counted as independently auditing itself; the positive witness is Security and distinct reviewer raw-code inspection of Coder-produced material. Mandatory QA in the ordinary production chain would not alone establish S3: the dedicated source-read and PM corrective return are decisive here.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No standing environment/future scanning → adaptation-option judgment → return into currently deployed organizational capability is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Evolving dependency vulnerabilities, new coding models, project needs and future tool capabilities could require adaptation, but inspected loops address a currently approved task or update ordinary memory. - Decisive decision or feedback right: PM R&amp;D path can explore user-specified PoCs and memory persists lessons; no autonomous role is shown deciding the orchestrator&#39;s prospective capability policy based on external future-relevant distinctions and returning it to S3 as an adaptation decision. - Decision owner: User for selecting model/configuration and proposing future projects, PM/Coder for the present R&amp;D or implementation task. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: CollectMemory, finalise-memory hooks, model config/provider fallback, project-context collection and R&amp;D PoC loops. - Closure path: Memory can inform next user task and PM may produce a PoC, but no ongoing future-environment model selects a new capability program and installs it in present operating policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Project-specific innovation, memory storage and re-running task-review do not independently constitute S4 external-prospective strategy. - Evidence: project context and recalled memory; R&amp;D task PoC loop; task completion/memory. - Basis: Bounded negative. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A user could explicitly order future-oriented research, but this is a bounded S1 project goal until an operating capability-adaptation loop is shown.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No distinct first-party ultimate organizational identity/policy dispute, legitimate final authority and binding operational return. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Safety concerns, task scope and human preferences constrain what code can be written, but routine task acceptance is not a constitutional choice for the system&#39;s identity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Human gates approve/reject task specifications, research and implementation plans; PM R&amp;D can propose ending, and Security may flag must-fix. These are project authorization/content decisions, not an established ultimate identity-policy authority loop. - Decision owner: Human user sets project scope and authorizes work; PM acts within delegated task scope. No S5 owner/path at chosen installed project recursion is identified. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: gateCh, regenerateCh, artifact approvals, security review findings, configured role instructions, model-provider permissions and audit reports. - Closure path: Approval releases task execution or re-planning within the same user task; source does not show identity-level disputes being escalated, decided and converted into durable governing policy for later organizational operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: TaskSpec and human approval are not ultimate policy governance by name, and security must-fix is a current operational safety issue unless it changes final organizational identity rules. - Evidence: task spec negotiation/approval; human plan approval; run gates. - Basis: Bounded negative. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: User governance over a broader software organization can be legitimate, but this project/task&#39;s approval gate has not been shown to adjudicate ultimate identity disputes.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/3eb14ad95676a95c0672d7ccaa1777882f752f31/assessments/ai/ai-coding-agents-orchestrator.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 3eb14ad95676a95c0672d7ccaa1777882f752f31\">3eb14ad &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-18T21:20:13+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/bradygaster/squad\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Squad</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-18\">2026-09-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/bradygaster/squad/tree/2099faf51c08a912c359209447011b06decf0565\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 2099faf51c08a912c359209447011b06decf0565\">2099faf &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A. - Function: specialist agents autonomously implement, test, document, review or otherwise produce project outcomes in their bounded domains. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repository/task uncertainty, tool results, implementation choices and domain-specific work state. - Decisive decision or feedback right: each spawned specialist chooses local work actions within its charter/task boundary. - Decision owner: model-driven specialist agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-agent contexts, charters, histories, tools and isolated/background execution modes. - Closure path: agent work changes repository/task state and returns labeled results into the team workflow. - Basis /\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A. - Distinct S1 units: multiple specialist agents executing work in parallel or in dependency chains. - Specific interference / oscillation: parallel agents can have data dependencies, circular dependencies or modify shared systems in ways that create conflicting implementations; the documentation explicitly warns and supplies design-review/dependency handling for these conditions. - Coordination relation: the Coordinator performs dependency analysis, chooses fan-out versus synchronous sequencing, detects circular dependencies and mediates resolution; multi-agent shared-system work can auto-trigger a Design Review so agents surface interfaces/risks/contracts before continuing. - Decisive right / owner: the model-driven Coordinator/Lead chooses the coordination response within the first-party team protocol rather than merely relaying messages. - Supporting mechanisms: routing tables, shared .squad/ state, background/sync execution and concurrency limits. - Closure path: coordination decisions alter later S1 ordering, participation, shared decisions and execution constraints before work proceeds. - Why not generic routing: the positive mapping rests on explicit dependency/cycle/shared-system conflict regulation, not named/domain dispatch alone. - Basis /\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P). - Autonomous mode function: regulate live whole-team commitments, assignment, concurrency, intervention and exception handling. - Whole-system current view: Coordinator/Ralph consume team state, available issues, recent decisions, work status and agent capabilities. - Autonomous decisive right / owner: Coordinator/Ralph-backed agent logic selects work, chooses which members execute it, decides parallel/sync structure, monitors execution and applies/recommends intervention; reviewer rejection can force reassignment rather than letting an S1 self-repair indefinitely. - Supporting mechanisms: routing manifests, orchestration logs, watch polling, status/health surfaces and tiered recovery enforcement. - Autonomous closure: decisions change assignment/execution/recovery state and subsequent team operation. - Parent mode: when current control exceeds autonomous authority, the first-party protocol pauses/routes to a human member or escalates to the user. If all capable agents are reviewer-locked, the Coordinator offers parent choices such as manual fix, unlock with guidance or close; the returned human decision changes subsequent current operation. - Parent owner: legitimate human team member/user at the project recursion. - Why A(P): autonomous current-control closure and a distinct operationally closed human-parent mode are both first-party supported; they are alternative ownership modes, not simultaneous dual control. - Basis /\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A. - Claim being audited: that an S1 agent&#39;s submitted implementation/work is acceptable for correctness, quality, architecture/security or test criteria. - Ordinary reporting path: the producing agent returns its result/history through the Coordinator. - Complementary access path: a separate Lead/Tester reviewer inspects the work under reviewer authority rather than relying on the author&#39;s own success claim. - Independence boundary: on rejection, the original author is locked out from self-revision for that task; the Coordinator must reassign work to another agent or escalate, preventing the audited S1 from owning both judgment and corrective disposition. - Decisive feedback right / owner: the reviewer agent approves or rejects within its stated scope. - Closure path: approval permits progression; rejection records the finding, locks the author and routes correction to a different actor or parent authority. - Basis /\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: —. - Retrospectives, personal histories, shared decisions and reusable skills improve organizational memory and can change later behaviour, but the reviewed evidence is primarily internal/project-experience learning. It does not establish a distinct externally and prospectively oriented environmental-intelligence function that models changing future conditions, develops adaptation options and closes them into present capability through S3. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: composition and role identity of the team, durable project directives/conventions and final resolution of decisions that the organization may not override autonomously. - First-party parent path: during initialization Squad proposes a roster, but the human confirms/customizes it before materialization; later the user may add/remove/redefine members and establish durable “always/never” directives. - Ultimate authority: the documentation names the user as the final arbiter who can override any decision. - Return-to-operation path: parent roster/charter changes are materialized into .squad/team.md/agent charters/routing, while durable directives enter decisions.md; subsequent agents read the current team/decision state before working, so the returned parent decision governs later operation. - Why not autonomous S5: Coordinator/Scribe may propose or propagate policy-like material, but the reviewed first-party boundary does not give them legitimate ultimate authority over team identity or final policy against the user. - Why P rather than generic HITL: the evidence concerns organization identity/durable policy and includes a reconstructable parent decision → persisted rule/team definition → subsequent-operation closure. - Basis /\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/96a771605cae253b024233012a564fafde18c029/assessments/squad.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 96a771605cae253b024233012a564fafde18c029\">96a7716 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-17T20:13:36+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/can1357/oh-my-pi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">oh-my-pi</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-17\">2026-09-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/can1357/oh-my-pi/tree/dbf3afad4894bde827d90f965e77b3fe1c5a95e5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision dbf3afad4894bde827d90f965e77b3fe1c5a95e5\">dbf3afa &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: the primary and delegated coding agents autonomously perform useful repository/tool work.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: the first-party task surface exposes a model-selected isolated-worktree mode specifically addressing sibling write/merge interference; the parent agent chooses which spawned work is isolated while the runtime enforces separation and merge/patch handling. Peer Hub messaging additionally returns coordination feedback between distinct S1s, but generic messaging alone is not the basis for the positive state.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: the autonomous primary agent receives a current control surface through hub: it can observe its active peers/background commitments, cancel owned jobs, steer/revive peers through messaging, and start/stop/restart shared supervised processes. These interventions alter current capacity/commitments while deterministic registries/job managers/brokers enforce the decisions. Separately, the human Agent Hub exposes the current subagent roster and lets the parent steer, revive and kill members, establishing a distinct parent-governed S3 mode.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: Advisor is a distinct reviewer actor with its own model/context/ToolSession and default independent workspace-inspection tools. It can challenge the primary trajectory and return findings that change subsequent execution through steering/blocker feedback. This supplies materially complementary access rather than merely re-reading the primary&#39;s final report.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: Advisor review, memory, model fallback and lifecycle maintenance concern present operation/quality; no separate prospective environment-facing adaptation loop was established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: approval modes, user settings and human session control define operational permissions and configuration. No first-party identity/ultimate-policy matter with legitimate S5 closure was established, so generic user authority is not published as S5=P.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/3c76cda1b3c51a33c84eeaa45ac3c296a54f8c3c/assessments/oh-my-pi.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 3c76cda1b3c51a33c84eeaa45ac3c296a54f8c3c\">3c76cda &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-31T23:17:23+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/the-open-engine/zeroshot\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">zeroshot</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-31\">2026-10-31</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/the-open-engine/zeroshot/tree/4c2ed7d5cf3dce968ef7634a2bb49f4382226637\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 4c2ed7d5cf3dce968ef7634a2bb49f4382226637\">4c2ed7d &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: implement user-requested code changes or run research experiments with model-directed tool/file actions and return artifacts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: arbitrary codebase tasks, repository state, model/tool feedback, tests and experiment outcomes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: instantiated worker/researcher agent selects substantive coding or experiment actions; zeroshot graph invokes and retains the role under a specific runtime binding. - Decision owner: the first-party configured graph node&#39;s autonomous agent role through a supported harness adapter; external harness internals retain lower-level execution but are not separately scored. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: model/harness binding, graph transitions, workspace/capsule, runner, provider session, SQLite ledger and bounded retries. - Closure path: task input → worker/researcher model/tool action → workspace artifact/experiment evidence → result returned to graph and, after review, revised worker action or accepted output. - Boundary reachability: zeroshot run --template software-change and auto-research are built-in supported native CLI/Docker graph modes with first-party runner and first-party role prompts. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: graph plumbing cannot implement semantic code changes or choose experiments without a model-driven worker; the autonomous operational actor is necessary. - Evidence: README; nativev2templates.rs; autoresearch.rs. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate interference between independently active run/postmortem cells that share a scoped lesson store and might overwrite contradictory or stale guidance. - Distinct S1 units: separately executing runs and their postmortem maintenance/discovery agents, each producing prospective repository/graph advice for the same shared memory scope. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrent updates to a lesson from stale snapshots, resurrecting a retired lesson, incompatible parallel advice replacing a newer record and duplicated overlapping recommendations. - Attenuating coordination relation: each specialist returns keep/adapt/retire/defer for frozen lesson IDs; host requires expected revisions and atomic publication, rejects conflicting writes, reloads the latest lesson snapshot, then re-invokes model reconciliation rather than accepting last-writer-wins. Same-scope overlap is consolidated; unrelated scopes cannot rewrite each other. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: a publication conflict suspends the stale mutation and returns refreshed data to maintenance agents; only successfully reconciled newer cards become injectable guidance into subsequent model/tool sessions. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the evidence identifies actual competing independent agent/run modifications of one shared knowledge resource and a conflict-specific reject/reconcile/return path, not merely a datastore or graph edge. - Disturbance / variety regulated: cross-run retained-guidance collisions and stale-write interference. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide how to reconcile a stale/overlapping scoped lesson so concurrent-run advice can coexist or supersede safely. - Decision owner: first-party optimistic-concurrency and scoped specialist reconciliation constructor; autonomous maintenance judgments are available but the general inter-run arbitration mode remains scoped/composed, not a universal S2 organization. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: versioned SQLite transactions, tombstones, stable IDs, scoped snapshots, conflict detection, specialist result schema and refresh/retry budget. - Closure path: concurrent postmortem decisions → CAS/version conflict → refreshed scope + specialist reconsideration → atomic accepted lesson revisions → future run retrieval or explicit failed reconciliation. - Boundary reachability: this is the supported local/Docker Active Memory path described in guide, implemented in postmortem.rs and store.rs. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the first-party mechanism explicitly constructs a recoverable S2-specific path; it does not establish broadly agent-owned coordination of every simultaneous run, so the conservative publication is C. - Evidence: Active Memory; postmortem.rs; store.rs. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: conflict witness is shared retained lessons across runs, not all inter-agent workflow interference. Worker/reviewer fanout and graph scheduling alone are not used as S2 proof.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: manage the current auto-research program and its experiment/review/correction choices across distinct scouting and research S1 units; not all unrelated zeroshot installations. - Whole-system current view: one auto-research run&#39;s three scout proposals, current experiment/report, reviewer verdict and feedback, retained candidate and bounded iteration state. - Current-control decision scope: select which current approach/experiment to execute, revise work from review evidence, retain accepted result or stop a blocked/inconclusive round. - Disturbance / variety regulated: competing scout directions, experimental failure, reviewer rejection and incomplete current research commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the model-driven researcher chooses experiment and revision priorities from scouts and review, while native graph enforces maximum iterations and terminal decisions. - Decision owner: first-party instantiated researcher Agent in the built-in auto-research graph; graph scheduler supplies deterministic execution constraints, and fresh reviewer provides challenge. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Par scout fan-in, researcher continuation session, bounded inner review loop, current state/feedback fields, SQLite run ledger and optional push gate. - Closure path: three model scout proposals → researcher decision/experiment → fresh reviewer evidence → same researcher revises or accepts → next round&#39;s current plan/retained result. - Boundary reachability: auto-research native template with shipped researcher instructions is built-in, not a user-authored hypothetical graph. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model researcher owns current experiment selection and corrective judgment; removing it leaves fixed fan-in/limits but not the substantive cross-scout control choice. - Evidence: README auto-research; autoresearch.rs; researcher.md. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: S3=A is specific to the built-in multi-agent research-program mode. In software-change, fixed graph control and reviewer gates alone are not credited as an independent model-driven S3 controller.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: test the implementer&#39;s outcome through fresh independent acceptance/code reviewers, then force rework on rejection before delivery or successful closure. - Claim being audited: worker&#39;s code meets requested acceptance criteria and code-quality/correctness expectations, not merely that the worker completed a turn. - Ordinary reporting path: worker writes/edit files, returns output and claims completed implementation in its retained session. - Complementary access path: separate acceptance and code reviewer nodes run in parallel against the shared workspace, each with its own fresh model session and rubric; their structured verdict/diagnostic inputs are not copied from the worker&#39;s self-report. - Independence boundary: both reviewers start fresh on every pass while implementation/repair uses the same worker session; the native graph gives reviewer roles separate identities and returns both findings. - Who acts on findings: enforced graph routes rejected reviewer evidence back to original worker; the next pass repeats both independent reviewers. Delivery/CI conflicts may also return through controlled repair and re-review. Acceptance cannot bypass guards with a prose-only assertion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incorrect self-reported success, acceptance gaps, code defects, regression and unreviewed delivery changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: separately model-driven reviewer decides accepted/rejected criteria and can prevent progression, triggering another worker repair round. - Decision owner: first-party instantiated independent reviewer/verifier agents for the standard software-change mode; trusted native graph gates/runner enforce their verdicts. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: typed graph signals, Par reviewer join, guard routing, bounded global loop budget, retained worker session, fresh reviewer sessions, run ledger and CI/delivery gates. - Closure path: worker code → fresh acceptance+code review → accepted or rejection diagnostics → worker repair → new review pass → accepted artifact/delivery or bounded honest failure. - Boundary reachability: the default software-change template and review/repair continuity tests demonstrate a standard operational path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: reviewers independently choose substantive feedback; graph machinery supplies independent session enforcement and corrective return, not semantic acceptance decisions itself. - Evidence: README; nativev2templates.rs; continuity.rs. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: independence is of decision sessions/evidence roles, not guaranteed model-provider diversity or exhaustive correctness. User-specified graph/model settings can alter actual review coverage.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: discover and reconcile costly recurring errors and outdated procedural knowledge so later graph/repository agent work is better adapted to changed evidence. - External distinction: real repository files and tool receipts, repeated costly failures/rejected review loops, current scope content and changed advice provenance, rather than the just-finished worker&#39;s optimistic summary. - Future / prospective distinction: determine which recurring or newly learned guidance will remain useful across subsequent runs, adapt stale lessons to current source and retire obsoleted/conflicting advice. - Adaptation option generated: graph and repository discovery specialists propose new procedural cards; separate maintenance specialists keep/adapt/retire/defer all supplied lessons, consolidate overlaps and identify stable recommendations. - Path back into current capability / S3: versioned accepted cards are stored per graph/node/repository and injected through native pre-tool hooks or graph instructions in future sessions, changing model choices in later S1/S3 work without rewriting the authored graph or policy. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repetition of expensive mistakes, stale memory, changed code/graph context and loss of useful experience between runs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: maintenance/discovery model specialists judge evidence-backed guidance changes; host validates cost/provenance, revisions and scope before publication. - Decision owner: standard-distribution postmortem model specialists in separate sessions, automatically enabled by default when Active Memory scopes and postmortem remain enabled; fixed validation/SQLite machinery enforces their decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool receipt capture, post-run outcome latch, per-scope indexes, strict cost threshold, version/CAS, bounded context, admission validation, tombstones, retrieval budgets and native hooks. - Closure path: run evidence/tool receipts → postmortem specialist review/discovery → validated revised scoped lessons → durable store → future run retrieval/injection → later model work and next postmortem reassessment. - Boundary reachability: the built-in Active Memory guide explicitly documents default enabled local/Docker paths; postmortem.rs, native supervisor, retrieval and hooks are source-backed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: specialist agents exercise context-sensitive keep/adapt/retire/discover discretion from actual evidence; host-only ranking/CAS cannot independently decide what lesson remains useful for future work. - Evidence: Active Memory; postmortem.rs; reconciliation tests; hooks.rs. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: postmortem may abstain when evidence is inadequate, can fail independently of completed main graph, and requires working model/harness hooks. Evidence of a closed adaptive mechanism is not proof that a given lesson causally improves future task quality.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no distinct legitimate ultimate-identity/policy conflict adjudicator and authoritative return loop exists within a graph-run organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: runtime scopes, model choices, graph contracts, delivery authorization and user approvals are fixed constraints rather than identity-level questions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no agent or parent actor is first-party delegated ultimate purpose/identity choice at this recursion. - Decision owner: user/operator defines graph, tasks, credentials, publishing authority and scope; native runtime enforces these settings. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: graph validation, authored runtime plan, capsules, limits, run ledger, Git/CI policy and memory namespace controls. - Closure path: an operator can change configuration for later runs, but no outstanding identity-policy matter is routed to a legitimate S5 authority and returned through an explicit governance closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: graph profile names, model persona, user delivery ratification, or managed platform policy do not by themselves constitute identity or ultimate-policy decisions. - Evidence: README; graph reference; Active Memory. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/0f8636f6b26e2e8f854dc02dd6102ef0ac8a32ec/assessments/ze/zeroshot.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 0f8636f6b26e2e8f854dc02dd6102ef0ac8a32ec\">0f8636f &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-31T22:33:05+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/desplega-ai/agent-swarm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Agent Swarm</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-31\">2026-10-31</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/desplega-ai/agent-swarm/tree/65e0ab6bb1f50f796b9c226e2bb34b5041cbaa66\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 65e0ab6bb1f50f796b9c226e2bb34b5041cbaa66\">65e0ab6 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Operational delivery by distinct coding/research/review workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Open-ended implementation/research requests, uncertain tool results and task failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose task-local investigation, tool actions, implementation and completion/progress response. - Decision owner: The model-driven operational worker in the configured provider session. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Runner prompt assembly, provider adapters, MCP tools, task persistence and runtime limits transport/enforce worker decisions. - Closure path: Task → assembled context → worker tool/action choices → progress/result → task and lead follow-up. - Boundary reachability: The supported self-hosted composition runs official lead and worker templates through provider adapters; the cited task/prompt paths are shipped deployment surfaces, not this repository&#39;s contributor agents. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the model worker leaves scheduling/storage, not the same discretionary investigation or implementation. - Evidence: docker-compose.example.yml; src/commands/runner.ts (adapter.createSession and task handling); templates/official/coder/CLAUDE.md; src/prompts/base-prompt.ts. - Basis: Pinned first-party source, supported deployment example and operational instructions; no live execution claim. - Confidence: High for the bounded source path; no measured reliability claim. - Caveats: Provider access is required; the assessment does not import unexamined provider governance or claim successful business output.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Attenuate interference among concurrent S1 workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New requests arrive through different ingresses while related work remains in flight, risking duplicate or contradictory implementation/review. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Interpret sibling intent, coordinate with named workers, avoid duplicate action, and choose compatible task work. - Decision owner: Lead and operational model workers own the coordination judgment; the sibling detector does not decide task semantics. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: contextKey lookup, in-flight task status, original-intent rendering, messaging/task tools, same-agent parent-session selection and lead no-duplicate rules. - Closure path: Same-context sibling discovery → task prompt lists owners/status/original requests and instructs coordinate/do not duplicate → agent queries/messages/reuses ongoing work → subsequent assignment/action changes. - Boundary reachability: The supported self-hosted composition runs official lead and worker templates through provider adapters; the cited task/prompt paths are shipped deployment surfaces, not this repository&#39;s contributor agents. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Sibling context intentionally exposes this inter-worker disturbance; the agent decides what overlaps and what should change. - Evidence: src/tasks/sibling-awareness.ts; src/tasks/sibling-block.ts; templates/official/lead/CLAUDE.md rules 2, 7, 8; src/tools/send-task.ts. - Basis: Pinned first-party source, supported deployment example and operational instructions; no live execution claim. - Confidence: High for the bounded source path; no measured reliability claim. - Caveats: Structural disturbance witness, not a claim that every collision is prevented. Incorrect/missing context keys can miss related work. - Distinct S1 units: Separately assigned coder, researcher and reviewer workers in the official multi-worker deployment. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Concurrent same-context tasks from multiple ingresses can duplicate or conflict with each other&#39;s unfinished work; same-PR reviews can be double-assigned. - Attenuating coordination relation: Named sibling tasks and status are supplied to the new worker; explicit coordinate/do-not-duplicate instructions plus task inspection/messaging let it adjust alongside lead deduplication. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Coordination changes which work a new task performs and whether lead creates/routes another task; same-agent continuation can resume existing context. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The first-party renderer explicitly detects concurrent related work, describes its original intent and instructs interference attenuation. Generic mailboxes or dependsOn alone are not the witness.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Control the team&#39;s inside-and-now commitments and recovery. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Stalled sessions, failed workers, unclaimed recovery pins, affinity-starved tasks and abandoned commitments across the team. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Assess current state and choose reroute, retry, cancel, recreate or intervention rather than blindly continue. - Decision owner: The official lead agent owns discretionary current-control decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Heartbeat preflight and code triage detect/escalate; snapshots, task creation, steer/cancel/send tools enforce the selected decision. - Closure path: Whole-team status and standing orders → lead heartbeat/reroute-decision task or worker-failure follow-up → lead investigates and chooses corrective task/resource action → task service changes subsequent worker operation. - Boundary reachability: The supported self-hosted composition runs official lead and worker templates through provider adapters; the cited task/prompt paths are shipped deployment surfaces, not this repository&#39;s contributor agents. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Automatic retry thresholds and transactions expose exceptional state; lead must choose the organizational response after those mechanisms cannot settle it. - Evidence: src/heartbeat/heartbeat.ts; src/heartbeat/templates.ts; templates/official/lead/HEARTBEAT.md; src/tasks/worker-follow-up.ts; src/tools/templates.ts task.worker.failed; src/tools/steer-task.ts; templates/official/lead/CLAUDE.md. - Basis: Pinned first-party source, supported deployment example and operational instructions; no live execution claim. - Confidence: High for the bounded source path; no measured reliability claim. - Caveats: A parent can administer deployment, but isolated configuration or approval is not counted as a distinct evidenced parent S3 mode. - Whole-system current view: Gathered agents, active/pending/failed tasks and stalled sessions, combined with lead standing orders and full task-detail access. - Current-control decision scope: Team commitments and recovery: cancel obsolete work, reassign eligible workers, steer current execution, recreate still-needed failed work and avoid retry storms.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Complementary audit of an operational code-delivery claim. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A coder can report completion while code contains security/race/state-machine faults or inadequate evidence/tests. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Form an independent review judgment with blocking findings/request-changes and return corrective requirements. - Decision owner: A distinct official reviewer model session owns audit judgment; coder/lead own correction and subsequent work routing. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Separate worker environment/task, direct git checkout, GitHub review/CI access, task completion/follow-up and parentTaskId continuity. - Closure path: Lead routes a review to reviewer → reviewer independently clones/checks out actual PR code and examines tests/CI → blocking findings/request changes → coder resolves feedback with parentTaskId continuity; lead can route corrective work. Human approval remains a separate merge requirement. - Boundary reachability: The supported self-hosted composition runs official lead and worker templates through provider adapters; the cited task/prompt paths are shipped deployment surfaces, not this repository&#39;s contributor agents. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The reviewer directly inspects operational evidence in a separate role/session and decides substantive findings, beyond the implementer&#39;s summary or deterministic CI verdict. - Evidence: docker-compose.example.yml; templates/official/reviewer/CLAUDE.md; templates/official/lead/CLAUDE.md routing/review rules; templates/official/coder/CLAUDE.md review-feedback rule; src/tasks/worker-follow-up.ts; src/tools/templates.ts. - Basis: Pinned first-party source, supported deployment example and operational instructions; no live execution claim. - Confidence: High for the bounded source path; no measured reliability claim. - Caveats: This is a supported role-based audit path when lead assigns review, not proof every task is audited. The ordinary 500-character completion follow-up alone is not complementary audit; shared credentials/model family are not institutional independence. - Claim being audited: Coder&#39;s proposed change is ready/correct and tests cover changed paths. - Ordinary reporting path: Coder progress/completion and a summary follow-up task to lead. - Complementary access path: Reviewer must clone repository and check out the PR branch, inspect actual code, tests and CI; not only read the coder&#39;s summary/diff. - Independence boundary: Separate reviewer worker/session and instructions, distinct from the producing coder; official deployment includes the reviewer. - Who acts on findings: Reviewer requests changes with concrete blocking fixes; coder resolves review feedback in the originating task context, with lead routing and subsequent review as needed.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: C - Function: Outside-and-then intelligence for the deployed team&#39;s own GTM campaign. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing discovery channels, search demand and content opportunities make the team&#39;s existing GTM plan less effective. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Turn external trend distinctions into candidate future campaign/content adaptations; adopter must still compose selection and implementation closure. - Decision owner: The configured lead produces options; deployment owner must supply campaign goal, integrations and the decisive adoption/return-to-S3 relation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: First-party parameterized weekly schedule prompt, GitHub/GSC integrations, configured GTM plan/state, admin report and saved research workspace. - Closure path: Configured own-campaign metrics → week-over-week search/referrer/content analysis → what-to-try-next and gap/opportunity options → report. Turning that report into an adopted current-capability change is intentionally not supplied as an automatic closed mode. - Boundary reachability: The supported self-hosted composition runs official lead and worker templates through provider adapters; the cited task/prompt paths are shipped deployment surfaces, not this repository&#39;s contributor agents. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A function-specific first-party prospective adaptation constructor exists, but no closed agent-owned or parent-owned adoption loop is established by the template. - Evidence: templates/schedules/gtm-weekly-review/config.json; templates/schedules/gtm-weekly-review/content.md; templates/official/discoverability-optimizer/SOUL.md. - Basis: Pinned first-party source, supported deployment example and operational instructions; no live execution claim. - Confidence: High for the bounded source path; no measured reliability claim. - Caveats: Optional template; requires own GTM goal, REPOURL/GSCPROPERTY and integrations. Ordinary customer research as a delivered task, generic memory, persona edits and arbitrary extensions are not S4 witnesses. - External distinction: Repository traffic/referrers/popular paths and Search Console queries, impressions, CTR and rankings for the deployment&#39;s configured campaign. - Future / prospective distinction: Week-over-week change, high-impression low-CTR/rank opportunities, content gaps and what to try next against the current GTM plan. - Adaptation option generated: Candidate SEO/content/campaign experiments to improve the team&#39;s own future reach. - Path back into current capability / S3: Report through configured admin/in-app channels and saved research workspace intentionally exposes options to the deployment; actor authority and adoption/execution closure must be composed, so C rather than A/P.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Identity and ultimate-policy adjudication at deployed-team recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A prospective conflict about the team&#39;s constitutive purpose or ultimate policy would require authoritative settlement. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No function-specific decisive identity/ultimate-policy arbitration and return loop is supplied in the inspected deployed surfaces. - Decision owner: No in-boundary S5 owner established; operator configuration and lead profile editing are not sufficient evidence. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SOUL/IDENTITY/CLAUDE persistence, update-profile, repo guidelines, permission/budget and extension activation controls. - Closure path: Observed paths persist persona, operational checks and permissions; they do not reconstruct a disputed identity/ultimate-policy matter being authoritatively settled and governing future operation. - Boundary reachability: The supported self-hosted composition runs official lead and worker templates through provider adapters; the cited task/prompt paths are shipped deployment surfaces, not this repository&#39;s contributor agents. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Neither writable identity files nor final human say on individual merges establishes the S5 function. - Evidence: docs-site/content/docs/(documentation)/architecture/agents.mdx/architecture/agents.mdx); src/tools/update-profile.ts; src/tools/repos/update-repo.ts; templates/official/lead/CLAUDE.md; src/prompts/base-prompt.ts; docs-site/content/docs/(documentation)/guides/extensions.mdx/guides/extensions.mdx). - Basis: Pinned first-party source, supported deployment example and operational instructions; no live execution claim. - Confidence: High for the bounded source path; no measured reliability claim. - Caveats: Bounded absence in this deployed distribution, not an impossibility claim for a separately composed organization&#39;s governance.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/548479149bdce4a226ecd139c0a8c4f7f244b2c7/assessments/ag/agent-swarm.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 548479149bdce4a226ecd139c0a8c4f7f244b2c7\">5484791 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-19T03:07:25+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/gastownhall/gastown\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Gas Town</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-19\">2026-09-19</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/gastownhall/gastown/tree/649b832b7672bc7a2dbef26f5983aba6198b819b\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 649b832b7672bc7a2dbef26f5983aba6198b819b\">649b832 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously implement bounded software-engineering issues in project repositories and return completed branch/work results. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task ambiguity, repository state, implementation choices, tool/test observations, local blockers and discovered defects within an assigned issue. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the substantive implementation/research/debugging actions required to satisfy the hooked issue and decide when the bounded work is ready to submit through the prescribed completion path. - Decision owner: the model-driven Polecat worker agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: isolated git worktrees, hooked Beads assignments, role/formula context, tool-capable model runtime, direct issue state updates and gt done completion plumbing. - Closure path: Mayor/runtime hooks an issue to a Polecat → Polecat executes autonomously in its isolated worktree → commits/pushes and runs the completion protocol → branch/result enters the downstream Witness/Refinery or configured PR path. - Boundary reachability: Polecat role templates, worktree provisioning, hook state and completion commands are shipped first-party Gas Town surfaces at the pinned revision; the external model host supplies inference but does not replace Gas Town&#39;s first-party worker contract. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the role and formula constrain execution, but the model-driven worker chooses substantive code/tool actions and how to solve the assigned issue. - Evidence: internal/templates/roles/polecat.md.tmpl, README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate integration interference among independently produced Polecat branches so concurrent work does not collide on a stale common baseline or land in an incompatible order. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple autonomous workers can finish branches from earlier target states; parallel/direct merging can create conflicts, invalid dependency order or inconsistent integration as the target branch moves after each accepted change. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next branch/merge order using priority, dependencies and timing; rebase it against the current target; resolve or reject conflicts; and require each subsequent branch to rebase against the newly advanced target. - Decision owner: the model-driven Refinery agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-rig merge queue, MERGEREADY protocol, isolated worker branches, sequential-rebase rule, target-resolution rules and merge-request state. - Closure path: multiple Polecat outputs enter the queue → Refinery selects one and rebases it on the current target → accepted merge advances the target → the next queued branch is rebased against that new baseline; unresolvable conflicts reopen the source issue and return through Witness for new work. - Boundary reachability: the canonical Refinery role and merge queue are shipped first-party operating surfaces for non-fork rigs; the role contract explicitly assigns merge/conflict/order judgment to the running agent. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic queue/state machinery exposes and enforces the integration surface, but the first-party Refinery contract gives the model agent discretion over ordering and conflict/failure disposition; this is not merely authored static sequencing. - Evidence: internal/templates/roles/refinery.md.tmpl, docs/design/mail-protocol.md, internal/refinery/batch.go. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: isolated worktrees by themselves would only be supporting interference attenuation; the A classification rests on the agent-owned sequential integration feedback loop. - Distinct S1 units: two or more autonomous Polecats completing separate issues/branches in the same rig or integration target. - Inter-S1 disturbance: their branches can be based on the same earlier target and therefore conflict, invalidate each other&#39;s assumptions or require dependency-sensitive ordering when integrated. - Attenuating coordination relation: Refinery serially selects, rebases and integrates branches against the current moving target instead of allowing parallel direct merges. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: failed/conflicting work reopens its source issue and returns through Witness for re-sling/rework; successful integration changes the baseline against which later queued work is evaluated. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the cited relation addresses a concrete interference mode among outputs of independent S1 workers — incompatible concurrent branch integration — and feeds conflict/failure results back into later operational work.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the town&#39;s current work commitments, dispatch, cross-rig allocation, escalations and active execution on behalf of the whole Gas Town workspace. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unassigned ready work, stalled or failed workers, competing/cross-rig work, blocked dependencies, changed priorities, integration/escalation problems and idle or misallocated execution capacity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose what work to dispatch and to which rig/worker, coordinate batch/cross-rig work, resolve escalations that Witnesses cannot handle, redirect priorities/integration decisions and intervene when current agents or rigs are stuck. - Decision owner: the model-driven Mayor agent in the standard autonomous mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: town/rig status, convoys, Beads dependencies, mail/escalation state, Polecat lists, hooks, gt sling, scheduler capacity support and Witness/Deacon feedback. - Closure path: current town/rig/convoy/escalation state becomes visible to Mayor → Mayor makes dispatch/coordination/intervention decisions → Gas Town creates/hooks workers or routes instructions/escalations → Polecat/Witness/Refinery/Deacon behavior proceeds under the changed current state and reports back. - Boundary reachability: Gas Town ships a dedicated Mayor session, first-party Mayor role template and control commands; Mayor is launched as an agent and connected to the same persisted work/communication surfaces used by the operational organization. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: scheduler and hook mechanisms constrain execution, but the global dispatch, cross-rig judgment and escalation-resolution rights are assigned to the model-driven Mayor rather than being fully predetermined by runtime code. - Evidence: internal/templates/roles/mayor.md.tmpl, internal/cmd/mayor.go, README.md, docs/design/escalation.md. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: the human Overseer can interact with or administratively operate Gas Town, but the reviewed evidence does not establish a sufficiently explicit distinct parent S3 operating mode comparable to a first-party approval/override lifecycle; ordinary human tasking and process controls are therefore not encoded as A(P). - Whole-system current view: Mayor has first-party commands and persisted state for overall town status, rigs, Polecats, active convoys, issue/dependency readiness, mail and escalations across the workspace. - Current-control decision scope: current work dispatch, worker/rig routing, batch/cross-rig coordination, escalation resolution, priority/integration judgments and intervention on stuck execution.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge a Polecat&#39;s completion claim before its branch is allowed to land and return corrective action when the integrated result is not acceptable. - Disturbance / variety regulated: producer self-verification can miss failures that appear only after rebasing onto the current target, integration conflicts, branch-caused test failures or untracked pre-existing failures that would otherwise be merged without an independent gate. - Decisive decision or feedback right: run the configured verification suite after rebase, decide whether failure is branch-caused or pre-existing, block/reject a failing branch, reopen its source issue and require returned rework, or allow a verified branch to merge. - Decision owner: the separate model-driven Refinery agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: MERGEREADY handoff from Witness, separate Refinery session/role, queue state, rebase onto current target, configured setup/typecheck/lint/build/test commands, mandatory handle-failures gate and MERGEFAILED/MERGED return protocol. - Closure path: Polecat completion passes through Witness → Refinery obtains the branch independently and rebases/tests it → pass permits merge; branch-caused failure reopens source issue, closes the merge request and notifies Witness → Witness/current-control machinery can re-sling corrected operational work. - Boundary reachability: the verification gate is embedded in the shipped canonical Refinery patrol contract and directly controls whether standard non-fork work reaches the target branch. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: tests provide evidence but do not decide the organizational response; Refinery independently interprets conflict/failure provenance and owns the block/reopen/merge judgment under a role explicitly separated from application development. - Evidence: internal/templates/roles/refinery.md.tmpl, docs/design/mail-protocol.md, internal/templates/roles/witness.md.tmpl. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: the same Refinery actor also owns S2 integration ordering, but the S3 claim uses a distinct decision right: independent acceptance/challenge of a producer completion claim and corrective return, not merge sequencing itself. - Claim being audited: that a Polecat&#39;s submitted branch is integration-safe and satisfies the configured quality/test checks sufficiently to land on the target branch. - Ordinary reporting path: Polecat performs its own completion/self-verification and signals done; Witness processes completion/lifecycle state and sends MERGEREADY. - Complementary access path: Refinery independently checks the queued branch after rebasing onto the current target and runs the configured verification commands in its own merge-processing session. - Independence boundary: Refinery is a separate agent role and is explicitly forbidden from acting as the application developer; it does not simply accept the producer&#39;s self-report and can block landing/reopen work. - Who acts on findings: Refinery directly blocks or merges; on failure it reopens the source issue and notifies Witness, which can return the work to a new/current operational worker.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no materially separate first-party external/prospective adaptation function is established at the reviewed town boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: external ecosystem change, future project/runtime conditions, model/provider evolution and strategic capability adaptation were inspected, but no qualifying shipped S4 owner and closed return loop was found. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established that continuously or episodically distinguishes outside/future conditions, develops adaptation options and returns a selected organizational adaptation into present Gas Town capability/S3. - Decision owner: none established for qualifying S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Mayor strategic/integration judgments, shared memories, project/research documents, HOP/Gas City material, scheduler state and historical capability/ledger data can inform work but do not by themselves close S4. - Closure path: no qualifying outside/future sensing → adaptation option → present capability/current-control return path is established in the credited standard operating surfaces. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the Mayor&#39;s use of the word strategic concerns current architecture, priority and integration decisions in the reviewed role contract; without a distinct external/prospective sensing-and-adaptation loop it is not promoted to S4. - Evidence: internal/templates/roles/mayor.md.tmpl, internal/cmd/mayor.go, README.md, reviewed design/research surfaces. - Basis: explicit + absence review -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no materially separate first-party organizational identity/ultimate-policy closure is established at the declared Gas Town workspace boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user intent, architecture/priorities, role templates, directives/configuration and Overseer interaction were inspected, but no qualifying town-level identity dispute/policy authority with a closed S5 return path was established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for choosing or ratifying the ultimate purpose/identity/constitutional policy of the Gas Town organization as distinct from assigning work, configuring roles or exercising current operational control. - Decision owner: none established for qualifying S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: the human Overseer, Mayor interface, role/formula templates, project configuration, directives/overlays, rig lifecycle controls and user instructions shape the system but are not sufficient S5 ownership by themselves. - Closure path: no first-party identity/ultimate-policy decision lifecycle comparable to goal/constitution/strategy ratification and return into autonomous operation was found at the pin. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: naming the human as Overseer, routing strategic questions to Mayor, or allowing an operator to configure/stop/dock agents does not by itself establish S5; those paths are tasking, configuration or current control unless the ultimate organizational identity decision is explicit. - Evidence: internal/cmd/mayor.go, internal/templates/roles/mayor.md.tmpl, internal/templates/roles/deacon.md.tmpl, reviewed configuration/directive surfaces. - Basis: explicit + absence review -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/7e14b6da80b9c974758f325654da2d00a7785624/assessments/gastown.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 7e14b6da80b9c974758f325654da2d00a7785624\">7e14b6d &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-16T19:14:58+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Muvon/octomind\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Octomind</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-16\">2026-10-16</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Muvon/octomind/tree/fceb8cbe970d2ad097e59d266ae915a3ebb43779\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision fceb8cbe970d2ad097e59d266ae915a3ebb43779\">fceb8cb &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute user objectives through model-directed local/MCP tool actions in an adaptive session. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown source tree, changing command results, failed tests, tool errors, external API responses and task-specific observations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model chooses the next tool, argument and work step after receiving prior action output. - Decision owner: first-party specialist actor in Octomind&#39;s session/chat runtime; the selected external model supplies inference, not a separate vendor harness. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: native Rust chat session, MCP call dispatch, role/tool config, session history, guardrails, compression, supervision and provider client. - Closure path: task→session model request→model tool instruction→MCP/native executor→recorded tool result→subsequent model turn or terminal result. - Boundary reachability: ordinary installed CLI and piped/daemon session modes enter same first-party model/tool session path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: action choices depend on model reasoning about live observations, rather than being only a prewritten deterministic script; policy and tool mediation constrain choices but do not invent them. - Evidence: src/session/chat/session/apiexecutor.rs, src/session/chat/response/toolexecution.rs, src/mcp/core/functions.rs, README.md. - Basis: source-backed installed autonomous specialist. - Confidence: high for runnable first-party code structure, not model task performance. - Caveats: tap-specific tools, hosted model weights and endpoint success are not part of ownership attribution.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no separately demonstrated first-party inter-S1 interference-attenuation relation within the selected coding-session and optional specialist group. - Disturbance / variety regulated: nested/dynamic agents can contend for model capacity or shared files; concrete conflicts are plausible but not shown as a closed native feedback relation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inter-agent conflict-specific arbitration that changes affected S1 behavior after detecting interference; not established by inspected paths. - Decision owner: supervisor handles the whole session&#39;s plan; child delegation and MCP orchestration communicate tasks/results without proven distinctive inter-S1 collision arbitration. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: delegate, MCP agent invocation, monitored tool processes, role constraints, generic authorizer/cost bounds and session/workdir state. - Closure path: child task/return and supervisor&#39;s whole-current plan changes do not independently form an S2-specific conflicting-worker attenuation loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a plan owner, tool batch authorization, per-role access bounds or generic delegation do not prove shared-resource contention detection and corrective return between separate operational divisions. - Evidence: src/supervisor/delegate.rs, src/mcp/agent/functions.rs, src/mcp/orchestration/monitor.rs, src/supervisor/plan.rs. - Basis: bounded negative source inspection. - Confidence: moderate at selected recursion level. - Caveats: additional externally configured multi-agent topology might bring S2 evidence, but that would require a concrete first-party collision-specific path and return.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: separate model-based whole-current supervisor of active specialist plan/phase commitments. - Disturbance / variety regulated: stalled work, unverified phase completion, broken assumptions or changed trajectory within a multi-phase objective. - Decisive decision or feedback right: external supervisor decides when to create, advance, hold or revise the operative phase plan according to runtime action evidence and original user outcome constraints. - Decision owner: first-party out-of-band supervisor model planner; runtime validates and applies its structured decision. The specialist does not possess the plan mutation tool. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: plan signals, per-turn preparation, callsupervisorjson, PlanDecision, sidecarstart/advance/revise, injected authoritative plan notes and bounded error latches. - Closure path: runtime-observed action/phase evidence→external planner decision→persistent current plan transition or evidence-grounded hold→runtime-plan note to model-directed specialist→subsequent task decisions occur under revised current phase. - Boundary reachability: first-party supervisor plan is enabled in shipped default config for qualifying complex tasks; trivial/answer-only jobs intentionally bypass it. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: distinct model decides actual whole-task phase regulation and returned current commitments, not a fixed progress counter or human-only approval. A concerns this documented current-control mode only. - Evidence: src/supervisor/plan.rs, src/session/chat/session/apiexecutor.rs, src/mcp/core/functions.rs, config-templates/default.toml. - Whole-system current view: standing instructions, original task, active plan, phase trajectory, evidence ledger and outcome conditions, beyond a single pending model tool call. - Current-control decision scope: choose and revise current task phase commitments and prevent unjustified advancement despite specialist claims. - Basis: source-confirmed distinct autonomous supervisor decision/return. - Confidence: high for structural ownership and default enablement, without live demonstration. - Caveats: planner intentionally declines simple requests, failed model calls can leave bookkeeping unchanged, and no autonomy is imported from a third-party agent.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: out-of-band supervisor-model verification of producer specialist&#39;s claimed done/completion against independent runtime action evidence and artifacts, with returned corrective work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: false completion, missing tests, unsupported work claims and regressions hidden by optimistic specialist narration. - Decisive decision or feedback right: separately tasked verifier determines Pass/Gaps/Indeterminate, with challenged findings able to prevent verified completion and re-enter correction. - Decision owner: distinct Octomind supervisor verification model invocation (not the specialist&#39;s own status token); first-party runtime owns the binding verdict gate and reentry budget. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: runtime action ledger, readback output, task/claim, ground-truth Git state, structured decision schema, independent refutation, bounded reentry and learning outcome labels. - Closure path: specialist self-reports done→first-party runtime captures actions/artifacts→separate supervisor verifier judges them→Gaps returned to specialist for bounded correction or explicit unverified outcome; Pass finalizes verifiable claim/plan and logs verified outcome. - Boundary reachability: enabled supervisor/gate in packaged default TOML, called at qualifying actual completion claims in the session executor; not off-box CI or benchmark scoring. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: distinct model performs substantive correctness judgment with access to runtime-owned evidence not controlled by producer, rather than mere transcript logging or same-agent self-review. - Evidence: src/supervisor/gate.rs, src/session/chat/session/apiexecutor.rs, src/supervisor/detect.rs, config-templates/default.toml. - Claim being audited: requested coding task is actually completed and outcome conditions hold. - Ordinary reporting path: specialist done token, user-facing summary and tool outputs. - Complementary access path: separate supervisor builds task/evidence packet from session action ledger, Git state and tool readbacks; can independently request missing output. - Independence boundary: independent role/model call and runtime-furnished ledger rather than specialist self-report, though same configured model-provider infrastructure may serve both. - Who acts on findings: runtime applies completion gate, new specialist correction prompt or unverified handoff; successful gate finalizes plan and supplies outcome credit. - Basis: source-confirmed agent decision and binding first-party corrective return. - Confidence: high for wired audit constructor, live model quality untested. - Caveats: abstention/timeout is not evidence of passing; no claim that an independent reviewer actually ran in this assessment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A(P) - Function: when operator enables optional evolution, external and cross-session observations are synthesized into proposed changes of future-session skills/guardrails; first-party counterfactual evaluation determines trial/promotion/retirement of generated behavior. - Disturbance / variety regulated: recurrent user constraints, observed repeated failure patterns, newly learned project rules and changed usefulness/cost of optional capabilities across future sessions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: separate supervisor model may autonomously synthesize an advisory skill candidate from grounded lessons; a first-party counterfactual trial judges whether activating it improves future outcomes, promotes an effective candidate or retires weak/regressed behavior. Parent controls enablement, effectful trials and explicit approval/reject/rollback. - Decision owner: detached learning supervisor plus native promotion/pruning policy own the autonomous S4 path; operator supplies initial opt-in and effectful authorizations, so supplementary parent (P) is explicitly recorded. User approval is not confused with autonomous model judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: source-quote and outcome-grounded Lesson, per-project store + scope, synthesizeafterextraction/synthesizestoreifdue, schema verification/replay checks, candidate registry, Shadow→Trial→Active/Retired, held-out counterfactual control/treatment, initforsession and generated skill/guardrail runtime merges. - Closure path: verified previous-session evidence→deduped reusable lessons→separate supervisor chooses a capability candidate→native schema/replay validation→shadow sample of future tasks without application→gated live treatment with counterfactual results→promoted skill/guard accessible to new sessions (or retired candidate)→actual future behavior changes and continues receiving evidence. - Boundary reachability: this is installed first-party [supervisor.learning.evolution] enabled = true mode, off by default, with code from extraction to runtime activation; no unpublished external R&amp;D process or user-installed extension is required. Effectful guards need explicit operator authorization; advisory promotion can be autonomous once opted in. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: prospective hypothesis/capability selection is produced by a model outside an individual current-task coding loop, then evaluated and fed back into later operational capabilities. It is not mere text-only memory recall or compaction. Both autonomous and parent-governed decisions have distinct documented roles. - Evidence: src/supervisor/learning/evolution/synthesize.rs, src/supervisor/learning/evolution/runtime.rs, src/supervisor/learning/evolution/registry.rs, src/supervisor/learning/extract.rs, src/session/context.rs, config-templates/default.toml. - External distinction: observations gathered across past sessions and project/role boundaries rather than an in-flight same task&#39;s tool result. - Future / prospective distinction: choose whether a machine-generated reusable skill/guard should persist as available behavior for later tasks. - Adaptation option generated: scope-bounded, verified native skill/guardrail candidate with a registry identity and explicit artifact path. - Path back into current capability / S3: runtime initforsession loads active generated skills and merges validated guardrails; promotion/retirement changes subsequent specialist behavior while gate/plan continue regulating current work. The operator may approve effectful candidates or revert the registry state. - Basis: first-party optional autonomous skill-generation→trial→activation and bounded parent rights. - Confidence: medium-high for source-confirmed pipeline; actual learning benefit not measured. - Caveats: evolution disabled in default config; without activation/grounded lessons or enough verified samples no positive rollout occurs. Distinguish confirmed code path from successful on-device trial. Parent permission for effectful native behaviors is mandatory.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no distinct legitimately constituted ultimate identity and organizational-purpose policymaker beyond scoped user/role constraints within an Octomind session. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tool misuse, role conflicts and evolved guardrail risks are addressed by operator instructions, bounded security policy and optional supervisor authorizer but do not constitute a separate S5 mandate. - Decisive decision or feedback right: interpret and resolve top-level organizational identity, constitutional purpose and ultimate contested policy with binding return to S3/S4; not established. - Decision owner: external operator provides objectives/configuration, first-party security controller enforces those constraints, supervisor authorizer may optionally judge specific tool intent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: role settings, hard guards, user-intent authorizer, command confirmation, generated-effectful authorization and user approval. - Closure path: a disallowed tool can be blocked, but no separately chartered ultimate organization policy/identity judgment returns to govern system aims; parent S4 guard approval does not create one. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: policy-as-code and delegated parent permissions are not sufficient to demonstrate a legitimately differentiated S5 above the task supervisor. - Evidence: src/supervisor/authorizer.rs, src/session/guardrails.rs, src/config/mod.rs, src/supervisor/learning/evolution/runtime.rs. - Basis: bounded negative. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: a user or adopting organization might own external S5, but that is not a first-party installed control loop at this recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/ae89fd48a07e9d2be6e67d313d1dfd935d61c95f/assessments/octomind.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit ae89fd48a07e9d2be6e67d313d1dfd935d61c95f\">ae89fd4 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-06T17:09:15+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/echoVic/blade-code\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Blade Code</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-06\">2026-10-06</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/echoVic/blade-code/tree/30f868411df4f7da095aadb7a2a8ca3310315613\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 30f868411df4f7da095aadb7a2a8ca3310315613\">30f8684 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously perform software-engineering and automation work through model-selected repository/tool actions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfamiliar code, implementation choices, tool/test/browser failures, model/provider variation, context pressure and bounded delegated work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose substantive coding/tool actions, interpret returned evidence, decide when to delegate or create a team and decide when the assigned operational objective is complete. - Decision owner: the active model-backed Blade Code agent and, for delegated bounded work, first-party subagent/team-member agents. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool registry/executor, permissions, context/session runtime, provider adapters, task admission, goal state, worktrees and cancellation. - Closure path: user/parent goal → agent chooses direct or delegated coding action → first-party runtime executes → tool/subagent/team evidence returns → agent revises work → completion is accepted when runtime completion gates permit it. - Boundary reachability: ordinary CLI/Web/Headless/ACP runtime uses the same first-party Agent/tool machinery; Task and Team tools are shipped model-callable surfaces. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic code executes and constrains actions, but the model chooses open-ended implementation, investigation, delegation and completion decisions. - Evidence: packages/cli/src/agent/Agent.ts; packages/cli/src/tools/builtin/task/task.ts; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: permissions and scheduling can delay/block actions without becoming the owner of the engineering decision itself.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate interference among concurrent operational teammates by assigning dependencies/ownership and isolating write-capable workstreams while coordinating peer progress. - Disturbance / variety regulated: duplicate task claiming, dependency-order violations, concurrent workers editing overlapping workspace state, and peer work that must be synchronized before downstream tasks proceed. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the team members, task partition, dependency graph, initial assignments and whether work benefits from parallel specialization/peer review; use peer messages to adjust coordination as execution unfolds. - Decision owner: the model-backed team lead through TeamCreate/SendMessage, with deterministic task-graph/worktree mechanisms enforcing the selected relation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TeamTaskGraph, atomic claim, automatic unblock, durable peer mailbox, managed worktree isolation for write-capable members and worktree lifecycle/merge machinery. - Closure path: lead identifies separable/interdependent work → creates roles/tasks/dependencies → teammates claim only unblocked work and write-capable members default to isolated worktrees → completion/unblocking/messages update peer execution → downstream units proceed under the adjusted coordination state. - Boundary reachability: TeamCreate, TeamTaskClaim, SendMessage and managed teammate worktrees are shipped first-party runtime tools, not documentation-only concepts. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the runtime enforces claims, dependencies and isolation, but the lead agent chooses the substantive organizational partition and dependency structure. - Distinct S1 units: persistent model-backed teammates/subagents that own bounded implementation or review outcomes. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrent workers can duplicate work, violate ordering, or collide through shared writes; managed worktrees and dependency/claim rules exist specifically to reduce those interactions. - Attenuating coordination relation: lead-selected DAG/assignment plus atomic task claiming, automatic dependency unblocking, durable peer messaging and worktree isolation for writers. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: completed tasks unblock dependent work; peer messages enter running teammate turns; task ownership/claim state changes which unit may work next; isolated results are later integrated rather than concurrently overwriting the same checkout. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the mechanisms regulate concrete interaction among simultaneously viable operational workers rather than merely transporting a delegated prompt. - Evidence: packages/cli/src/tools/builtin/team/teamTools.ts; packages/cli/src/agent/teams/TeamTaskGraph.ts; packages/cli/src/agent/subagents/SubagentWorktreeLifecycle.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: atomic claiming and worktree mechanics are deterministic support; autonomy is credited to the lead&#39;s model-owned organizational decisions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: maintain a live whole-team view and regulate current operational commitments while the multi-agent organization is running. - Disturbance / variety regulated: stalled or unwanted teammates, changed current priorities, task/member status changes, blocked downstream work and a team that should be stopped after synthesis or failure. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect the live team/member/task graph, steer a running teammate with a direct message, broadcast a changed instruction, and terminate running teammates by deleting the current team. - Decision owner: the model-backed team lead through TeamStatus, SendMessage and TeamDelete. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentSessionStore-backed live member state, durable task graph, TeamMailbox, running-turn message delivery and team/member cancellation. - Closure path: lead inspects TeamStatus/current graph → detects a current execution need → sends targeted/broadcast steering or terminates the team → running teammate/session state changes → later TeamStatus/results reflect the intervention and the lead proceeds accordingly. - Boundary reachability: TeamStatus/SendMessage/TeamDelete are shipped model-callable first-party tools available to the team lead. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: live state storage and cancellation transport are deterministic, but the lead model interprets whole-current state and decides the substantive intervention. - Whole-system current view: TeamStatus returns live member state from AgentSessionStore together with durable task-graph state for the owned team. - Current-control decision scope: current peer steering, task/role supervision through the shared graph, and cancellation of running team commitments when work should stop. - Evidence: packages/cli/src/tools/builtin/team/teamTools.ts; packages/cli/src/agent/teams/TeamRuntime.ts; packages/cli/src/agent/teams/TeamMailbox.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic stop/cancel would not be enough alone; the positive claim rests on the combined live whole-team status surface plus targeted running-turn steering and commitment termination.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge qualifying implementation work with a fresh read-only verifier whose evidence can block completion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incorrect implementation claims, failing tests/types/lint, medium/high-risk defects, race/security issues and author self-verification bias. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independently execute allowed verification commands, inspect the authoritative changed-file scope and emit structured PASS/FAIL/PARTIAL findings. - Decision owner: the separately instantiated built-in verification agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: fresh subagent context, read-only tool restrictions, authoritative verification prompt, mutation-revision tracking, bounded retry gate and deterministic completion policy. - Closure path: main agent changes qualifying implementation → runtime requires fresh verification Task → independent verifier gathers direct project/tool evidence and emits verdict → FAIL/PARTIAL forces repair/re-verification; only fresh PASS for current mutation revision permits completion. - Boundary reachability: independentVerification is wired into the shipped main loop and explicitly requires the built-in verification subagent for qualifying changes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the verifier model owns the audit judgment from its own evidence path; deterministic runtime code enforces freshness and blocks completion when the verdict is insufficient. - Claim being audited: the main coding agent&#39;s claim that the current non-trivial implementation is correct and ready to finish. - Ordinary reporting path: the author agent&#39;s normal tool results, implementation narrative and self-checks. - Complementary access path: a separate read-only verifier runs project checks and reads changed files directly under an authoritative scope. - Independence boundary: fresh model-backed subagent, no write tools, no nested subagents, tool-evidence-only instructions and no reliance on the author&#39;s claimed test outcome. - Who acts on findings: the main agent must repair reported failures and invoke a new verification run; the runtime completion gate prevents success until the required PASS is fresh. - Evidence: packages/cli/src/agent/loop/independentVerification.ts; packages/cli/src/agent/subagents/verification.md; packages/cli/src/agent/subagents/builtinVerificationAgent.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic code-review skills are not used for this claim; the positive S3 path is the enforced built-in verifier.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party external-and-prospective adaptation loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no qualifying future/external condition is placed under a function that develops adaptation options and returns them into current S3 capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S4. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: auto-memory, compaction-time consolidation, provider health/recovery, web/browser tools, goals, schedules and plugin/skill configuration can affect later work but do not by themselves close S4. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S4 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: memory consolidation extracts preferences/conventions/lessons/debugging notes and injects retained project knowledge into later sessions; that is persistence/learning context rather than an evidenced outside-and-then option-development conversation with current S3. - Evidence: packages/cli/src/memory/AutoMemoryManager.ts; packages/cli/src/memory/MemoryConsolidation.ts; packages/cli/src/goals/executionFrontier.ts. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the runtime has substantial persistence and external tooling, but these do not satisfy the Methodology 0.3.6 external-and-prospective option loop without an adaptation owner.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy governance loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no identity-level or ultimate-policy conflict is routed to a distinct authoritative S5 owner. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S5. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: permission modes, workspace trust, system prompts, tool policy, plugin/MCP security and configuration constrain execution but do not create identity-governance closure. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S5 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Blade Code agents operate within developer/user-authored constraints and do not have authority to redefine the system&#39;s ultimate identity or governing principles. - Evidence: packages/cli/src/config/PermissionChecker.ts; packages/cli/src/security/WorkspaceTrustService.ts; packages/cli/src/prompts/sections.ts. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: operator permissions and configuration are legitimate constraints, not identity/ultimate-policy governance.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/37d2b8f891a1fec144fc3d87e36ec29a6c976a96/assessments/blade-code.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 37d2b8f891a1fec144fc3d87e36ec29a6c976a96\">37d2b8f &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-11T23:52:40+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/MaximeRobeyns/self_improving_coding_agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SICA</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-11\">2026-10-11</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/MaximeRobeyns/self_improving_coding_agent/tree/ed8275dca4d3c5dbf77229964351fe9b424797dc\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision ed8275dca4d3c5dbf77229964351fe9b424797dc\">ed8275d &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: First-party agent loops perform model-directed coding/source inspection, sandboxed shell operations, benchmark problem solving and independently executing delegated workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New bug/task descriptions, mutable repository content, tool errors, model output and local experiment conditions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model selects concrete coding steps and allowed tools after receiving tool outputs or prior subagent results. - Decision owner: Executing CodingAgent/ProblemSolver child model, MainOrchestrator&#39;s bounded solver actions and base agent tool-call continuation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent classes, tool parser and call graph, executecommand, file and edit tools, sandbox Docker process. - Closure path: Task → model-directed operations → actual tool/command outputs and artifact → subsequent step or returned result, with sandbox limits. - Boundary reachability: Packaged baseagent.agent runs MainOrchestratorAgent, which can create coding/model workers; shipped runner.py invokes that executable in isolated Docker containers. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Task-specific coding decisions are model-generated rather than only an external benchmark script. - Evidence: baseagent/agent.py; baseagent/src/agents/implementations/coder.py; baseagent/src/agents/agentcalling.py; baseagent/src/tools/executecommand.py; runner.py - Basis: explicit / structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Prevent distinct concurrent benchmark-running agent S1 workers from interfering through shared host HTTP ports and shared benchmark result-tracker mutation. - Distinct S1 units: Multiple Python/Docker child coding agents each solve separate benchmark problems through runbenchmarkswithjobqueue and processjob, independently maintaining a coding task loop. - Inter-S1 disturbance: If two independently executing benchmark agents bind the same host port 8080/808x, one container fails to start; concurrent writes to the same benchmark JSONL tracker may lose or corrupt result/progress state. - Attenuating coordination relation: runbenchmarkswithjobqueue allocates a unique workerid from bounded idqueue; processjob binds container host port 808{workerid}. Each benchmark uses asyncio.Lock around tracker start/result updates, preventing simultaneous writes to shared provenance. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Each worker occupies one reserved ID/port while running its model and returns ID only on exit; subsequent agent work waits for a safe slot or starts on another allocated ID; results can be safely saved and analyzed for next iteration. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The first-party ID lease and tracker lock address two named interference modes produced by distinct simultaneously acting model agents; the evidence goes beyond mere task assignment/queue routing. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Host port contention and cross-worker result-store writes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Allocate/release exclusive host port and serialize benchmark tracker mutations. - Decision owner: First-party deterministic idqueue and per-benchmark asyncio.Lock constructor, not an autonomous S2 mediator model. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Bounded maxworkers, unique Docker names, per-problem workdir mounts and benchmark data lock. - Closure path: Several runnable problem jobs → lease workerid/unique host port → parallel model sessions → guarded tracker write → release lease → next job. - Boundary reachability: Default runner.py uses this first-party benchmark job runner during evaluation of each agent iteration; positive S2 is bounded to this concurrent benchmark-agent mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Safe exclusive-resource scheduling is deterministic host-owned (C), not the meta agent&#39;s discretionary choice. - Evidence: runner.py; baseagent/src/benchmarks/base.py - Basis: structural -\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Model-driven executive direction of multiple current subordinate coding workers with shared task progress and corrective redirection based on their execution results. - Whole-system current view: MainOrchestrator owns the top-level task and receives the complete problem and a synthesized live execution tree from CallGraphManager after subordinate calls, including already attempted work/results and current subordinate execution state. - Current-control decision scope: MainOrchestrator selects/decomposes active subagent tasks, assigns complete subtask instructions, scrutinizes their returned evidence, changes the plan, commissions further workers/verification and decides when a coherent current task result is submitted. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Subagent failures, incomplete work, duplicate approaches, requirement mismatch and uncertain current progress. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Discretionary model-controlled reassignment, continuation/stop and verification of the current multi-agent goal; no reliance solely on fixed queue. - Decision owner: First-party MainOrchestratorAgent LLM (A) at parent/current multiunit recursion, separate from subordinate child-model operational work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: CallGraphManager, generated execution-tree event, model-driven agent calls, structured task results, cost/time governors. - Closure path: Current top-level intent → orchestrator decomposition → model worker runs → returned results plus direct callgraph operating state → revised plans/delegation and final task acceptance. - Boundary reachability: baseagent/agent.py constructs MainOrchestratorAgent and starts it as the initial executing actor in CLI and benchmark mode; role documented in the agent&#39;s first-party core prompt. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The parent model uses evidence and discretionary judgment to govern concurrent/successive independently thinking subagent units, rather than merely running a predefined list of subtasks. - Evidence: baseagent/agent.py; baseagent/src/agents/implementations/mainorchestrator.py; baseagent/src/callgraph/manager.py; baseagent/src/agents/agentcalling.py - Basis: explicit / structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Separate model-driven runtime monitor checks independent live callgraph/effect events for subagent nonprogress, looping and abnormal execution and can issue corrective notices or terminate faulty child work. - Claim being audited: Operational actors&#39; implicit assertions of continuing useful work and healthy nested execution; notably a child may be silently looping or failing while its parent awaits results. - Ordinary reporting path: Each coding child returns self-authored work and final text to its parent, potentially omitting stalled nested activity. - Complementary access path: An independently launched Overseer model periodically/event-triggeredly reads generateoverseerexecutiontree(CallGraphManager), a first-party event-derived live execution graph, not only the child/parent&#39;s narrative result. This is source-observed execution, although not an independently executed code quality benchmark. - Independence boundary: Overseer is a separate model invocation outside producer and supervising parent tool-delegation turn, with its own model option and timing/event observations. Shared first-party callgraph and provider limits remain; no external independent auditor claim. - Who acts on findings: Overseer itself publishes OVERSEERNOTIFICATION to an implicated agent/parent and calls CallGraphManager.cancelagent when model judge sets forcecancelagent; parent responds via next tool/model decision. It also adapts future monitoring cadence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Task nonprogress, recursion loops, stalled/erratic worker execution and hidden operational states. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model independently decides concern/notifiability, which actor to notify, whether to cancel, and whether future checks are time/event-driven. - Decision owner: Separate autonomous Overseer model, not a rule-only timeout checker. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: EventBus subscriptions, execution-tree renderer, OverseerJudgement schema, LLM check interval, notifications and cancellation path. - Closure path: Real child event and call graph → separate model Overseer diagnosis → notification or direct cancel → parent agent control loop sees issue and can reassign; subsequent oversight resamples. - Boundary reachability: baseagent/agent.py always instantiates and starts Overseer when running packaged agent; its monitoringloop subscribes to tool result/assistant events while root agent executes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The audit&#39;s judgment and direct cancellation/intervention are separate model decisions using a complementary event-derived operating view; not a producer self-review or passive observability dashboard. - Evidence: baseagent/agent.py; baseagent/src/oversight/overseer.py; baseagent/src/callgraph/reporting.py; baseagent/src/callgraph/manager.py - Basis: explicit / structural - Confidence: medium-high for wiring, medium for audit effectiveness - Caveats: Overseer is an execution-health complementary audit, not independent code correctness review. It may share model provider and callgraph evidence with producer; cancellation can be mistaken or delayed. ReviewCommittee is separately model-driven design critique but is not credited as direct runtime correctness audit.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: Model-driven future-capability adaptation: evaluate externally defined benchmark tasks and past agent-version results, generate and implement software architecture/tool changes for subsequent agent generations. - External distinction: Benchmark tasks and measured grader outcomes originate outside the current coding agent&#39;s self-report; the archive records past failures, costs and utility scores. Published literature/web exploration can inform new feature options in the meta-improvement reasoning workflow. - Future / prospective distinction: The meta agent is tasked to improve the next version&#39;s general coding competence, not just solve the currently evaluated benchmark problem or fix the present code task. - Adaptation option generated: MetaImprovementReasoningStructure directs archive analysis (working and failed features), a model-chosen new feature/strategy, code change proposal, multi-member read-only ReviewCommittee critique, model-directed implementation and planned tests. - Path back into current capability / S3: runner.py evaluates a version, selects a base version by stored benchmark statistics and confidence bound, copies code into next iteration, runs that version&#39;s model-driven improve mode on its own codebase, then rebuilds Docker and runs subsequent version benchmarks; resulting changed tool/prompt/coding policy becomes future subordinate S1 capability used by MainOrchestrator&#39;s current-work organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Long-term changes in measured coding benchmark performance and repeated feature failures, rather than current tool-loop transient errors. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model chooses which agent architecture/tool/behavior feature should be changed after comparing archived experience and review committee feedback, with real downstream implementation and retest. - Decision owner: Meta-improvement MainOrchestrator/CodingAgent and archive analysis model in the explicit self-improvement operating mode; version selection/testing is first-party deterministic support. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Benchmark registry, archive analyzer with utility/CI, selectbaseagent, independent model-driven ReviewCommittee, meta-improvement reasoning structure, agentchangelog.md, isolated next-agent directory. - Closure path: External benchmark/task outcomes → version-level archive → model diagnosis and novel future improvement option → separate committee critique → actual coding changes to next version → newly launched/evaluated agent iteration and updated archive. - Boundary reachability: Documented python runner.py default cycle executes benchmarks and then runmetaagentbenchmark, which launches baseagent improve mode. That mode integrates meta-improvement reasoning and committee. No external company service is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Future capability choice and proposal/refinement are model-authored, not merely a static benchmark-triggered template or heuristic version selector; first-party script closes deployment/evaluation. - Evidence: runner.py; baseagent/src/tools/reasoningstructures/metaimprovement.py; baseagent/src/agents/implementations/mainorchestrator.py; baseagent/src/tools/committeedesign.py; baseagent/src/agents/implementations/archiveexplorer.py - Basis: explicit / structural - Confidence: medium-high for model-driven improvement cycle, low for empirical net improvement - Caveats: A new iteration is produced even when benchmark gains are not established; selection uses observed statistics but does not prove monotonic improvement, safety or benchmark transfer. No production performance results inferred.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No evidenced legitimate ultimate-purpose/policy authority with identity-level deliberation and binding return across all future agent adaptations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Risk of benchmark Goodharting, security tradeoffs, resource spending and deciding which future changes are acceptable. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Humans configure objectives/budget, benchmark suite, model credentials and Docker trust. Committee can critique designs but cannot ratify ultimate identity or adjudicate mission change. - Decision owner: External experiment operator and developer-set prompt/policy boundaries; no dedicated legitimate S5 body. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ReviewCommittee &#39;taste maker&#39;, testing manager, fixed guards, benchmark score criterion, sandbox/budget limits. - Closure path: Committee reports recommendations and model may refine next feature; no identity-level issue/ultimate authority/legitimate binding policy return at S5 recursion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A sophisticated self-improvement S4 or design-review committee does not imply governing constitutional/organizational identity authority. - Evidence: baseagent/src/tools/committeedesign.py; runner.py; baseagent/src/agents/implementations/reviewcommitteemember.py; baseagent/src/config.py - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/3efb20aba48801604f7585275a138d80bcb905ee/assessments/sica.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 3efb20aba48801604f7585275a138d80bcb905ee\">3efb20a &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-18T22:28:43+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Snowflake-Labs/cocoplus\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CocoPlus</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-18\">2026-09-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Snowflake-Labs/cocoplus/tree/b252a8e7e09da6ca3178000d1fb5f48783dffc13\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision b252a8e7e09da6ca3178000d1fb5f48783dffc13\">b252a8e &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: specialist/persona agents autonomously perform the data-engineering/project transformations defined by the active CocoPlus goal/flow. - Disturbance / variety regulated: requirements, code/data artifacts, tool/Snowflake results, tests, implementation uncertainty and bounded project-state changes within each specialist remit. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose local implementation/analysis/tool actions needed to complete the assigned stage/iteration outcome. - Decision owner: model-driven CocoPlus specialist/producer agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: personas, Snowflake Coco host, phase/stage contracts, model-tier floors, isolated worktrees, artifacts and tool/policy hooks. - Closure path: specialists produce artifacts/evidence in their bounded stage; outputs are persisted and passed to later stage/review/current-control paths, affecting subsequent project work. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic gates/hook policy restrict actions but do not choose the specialist&#39;s substantive solution; the model-driven actor owns local operational judgment. - Evidence: README.md and CocoForge/CocoFlow documentation describe specialist agents executing build/test/review/project stages and autonomous long-running modes. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: regulate dependencies and destructive concurrent interference among specialist/producer S1 units while preserving useful parallelism. - Disturbance / variety regulated: dependency-invalid stage starts, concurrent producers touching overlapping file scopes, incomplete work contaminating another agent&#39;s context and oscillation between parallelism levels after failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide which approved-plan work can run simultaneously versus must wait, and adapt stage parallelism from current dependency/failure conditions. - Decision owner: model-driven CocoHarvest/Team Lead coordination actor operating under the first-party flow contract. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: dependency graph/gates, isolated worktrees, disjoint producer filescope, checkpoint artifacts, concurrency/retry budgets and deterministic dispatch guards. - Closure path: the coordination decision changes which stages/producers dispatch, which wait, and the scope they receive; dependency/checkpoint failures and disjoint-scope enforcement prevent unsafe execution until the coordination condition changes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: dependency/worktree mechanisms enforce safe boundaries, while the agentic harvest/lead path chooses the useful parallelization/coordination response within them. - Evidence: docs/features.html calls CocoHarvest the intelligence behind parallelism and describes dependency-aware waits/isolated worktrees/adaptive stage parallelism; AGENTS.md.template requires concurrent producer scopes to remain disjoint. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: plain phase sequencing is not credited; the positive witness is the concrete dependency/write-contamination disturbance with an agent-owned parallelism decision. - Distinct S1 units: two or more specialist/persona/producer agents executing separate CocoFlow/CocoForge/CocoFleet work. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping writes or dependency-invalid parallel stages can contaminate shared state, create conflicting outputs or cause downstream work to start from incomplete prerequisites. - Attenuating coordination relation: CocoHarvest/Team Lead chooses parallel versus waiting/dependency order; disjoint file scopes, isolated worktrees and checkpoint gates enforce that chosen separation. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: only admitted stages/producers dispatch, blocked dependencies remain waiting, and failure/regression signals can narrow parallelism before later execution. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the mechanism is explicitly tied to preventing cross-agent contamination and dependency conflict, not merely assigning or ordering tasks.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: regulate current whole-run commitments, specialist dispatch, iteration/milestone progression, budgets, recovery and live intervention. - Disturbance / variety regulated: failing iterations, budget pressure, stalled stages, specialist output disagreement, quality-gate failure, current run-policy constraints and exceptions requiring developer intervention. - Decisive decision or feedback right: autonomous mode — choose iteration plan/dispatch/synthesis/next milestone response and current recovery within hard bounds; parent mode — clear human-gated stages, steer/stop/resume or resolve a dispute when current-control authority is deliberately reserved to the developer. - Decision owner: autonomous mode — model-driven Team Lead/run coordinator; parent mode — developer/operator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: forge-state.json goal/iteration/phase/budget/team/history, milestone gates, run-policy snapshots, stall detection, retry budgets, AGENTSTOP/STEER, human-gate files and deterministic pre-tool policy hooks. - Closure path: autonomous lead decisions change specialist assignments, iteration state and next-stage progression; parent decisions clear/retain gates, resolve disputes or stop/steer the run, and the persisted state/policy governs subsequent agents. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Team Lead owns current orchestration within the established run boundary; hard hooks enforce constraints, while explicitly documented human gates/dispute/steering paths preserve a separate parent-owned mode rather than being attributed to the runtime. - Evidence: forge.skill.md assigns plan/dispatch/synthesis/milestone-gate decisions to Team Lead and persists whole-run state; AGENTS.md.template supplies AGENTSTOP/STEER, non-bypassable human gates and run-policy boundaries; docs/features.html supplies recovery/escalation/adaptive stage control. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: static policy enforcement by hooks is not credited as S3 ownership; the mapping depends on Team Lead discretion and explicit returned parent decisions. - Whole-system current view: current forge goal, iteration, phase, budget status, team, history, stage dependencies, evidence/critic status and run-policy snapshot. - Current-control decision scope: iteration plan, specialist dispatch, synthesis, milestone progression, current recovery/parallelism and developer-gated intervention.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge specialist/producer quality claims before milestone/signoff using a structurally separated critic path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: producer self-approval, rationalization, hidden quality regressions and acceptance of evidence that does not meet stated criteria. - Decisive decision or feedback right: issue PASS/REJECT quality judgment from the independent evidence presented to the critic and gate progression accordingly. - Decision owner: model-driven Quality Critic / read-only fleet critic. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: critic read-only role, lack of specialist reasoning traces, default-REJECT stance, producer/critic separation, required PASS signoff and milestone/refinement gates. - Closure path: critic verdict enters the milestone/flow state; rejection prevents acceptance/progression and drives another fix iteration/dispute, while PASS permits the governed continuation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the critic agent owns the quality judgment; read-only/separation/gating mechanisms preserve independence and enforce its consequences but do not produce the judgment. - Evidence: AGENTS.md.template states that the Quality Critic receives iteration outputs but not specialist reasoning traces, starts from REJECT and cannot be overruled by Team Lead; fleet mode separates producers from read-only critics and can require PASS; forge.skill.md places critic verdict in each milestone gate. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: routine tests/evidence gates are not the S3 witness; the mapping is specifically the independent critic path with complementary output-level access and non-overridable feedback. - Claim being audited: that specialist/producer iteration output meets the stated goal/quality/evidence criteria and is safe to accept for progression. - Ordinary reporting path: specialist/producer returns its output/artifacts into the Team Lead/flow iteration. - Complementary access path: separate Quality Critic/read-only critic receives iteration outputs/evidence without the specialist reasoning trace and evaluates them under its own criteria/default-REJECT stance. - Independence boundary: critic is separated from producer, read-only in fleet mode, lacks producer reasoning traces and cannot be overruled by Team Lead. - Who acts on findings: Team Lead/flow milestone control consumes PASS/REJECT; rejection forces another iteration, withheld signoff or dispute escalation before progression.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: C - Function: inspect externally changed model capability after an upgrade, determine how that changes the value of existing governance/harness rules and develop evidence-backed adaptation options for future operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a newer model may make standing rules/checks newly redundant, advisory or still necessary, creating risk of stale harness constraints or unsafe premature deletion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judge which recurring rule/check should remain load-bearing, degrade to advisory or be proposed as obsolete after model-capability change. - Decision owner: constructor path — CocoPlus supplies the S4-specific model-upgrade hygiene analysis, but the standard path does not autonomously close destructive rule changes; developer composition/authority is still required for returned adaptation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: $hygiene --model-upgrade, evidence collection across sessions/committed artifacts, retrospective/benchmark recommendations and prohibition on silent deletion. - Closure path: hygiene produces evidence-backed keep/advisory/obsolete recommendations intended to change recurring hooks/skills/standing context, but destructive adaptation is proposed rather than automatically applied; downstream developer action must complete the return into present capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an agent can perform the prospective analysis, yet CocoPlus intentionally withholds autonomous closure over the recurring harness rules; this is therefore a supplied S4 constructor path rather than A. - Evidence: cocohygiene/hygiene.skill.md explicitly defines a model-upgrade audit of governance rules/harness checks and classifies their future status while requiring evidence and proposing rather than silently applying deletions; AGENTS.md.template directs retrospective/hygiene/model benchmarking before changing recurring rules. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: generic retrospectives/learning are not credited as S4. The positive mapping is narrowly the external model-upgrade path; C reflects incomplete first-party adaptation closure. - External distinction: the capability/behavior of the upgraded external model relative to the assumptions under which current CocoPlus rules were created. - Future / prospective distinction: whether recurring governance/harness checks will remain necessary, become merely advisory or become obsolete for subsequent sessions/runs. - Adaptation option generated: retain the rule, downgrade its standing, replace/adjust it or propose removal when evidence shows it is obsolete. - Path back into current capability / S3: the recommendation targets recurring hooks/skills/standing context used by current runs, but developer action is still needed to apply the proposed structural change; this incomplete closure is why the state is C.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no distinct runtime identity/ultimate-policy function is established at the reviewed project/run recursion beyond developer-authored policies, covenants and safety boundaries. - Disturbance / variety regulated: autonomy covenants, hard stops, run policies, safety rules and human approvals constrain operation, but the evidence does not show a legitimate S5 identity/ultimate-policy conversation with closure separate from those constraints/current-control gates. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no material first-party S5 decision right is established. - Decision owner: none established for a qualifying S5 function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: covenant acceptance, run-policy snapshots, hard safety gates, human gates, policy engine, steering restrictions and persistent project configuration. - Closure path: these controls constrain subsequent operation, but no identity/ultimate-policy issue → legitimate S5 judgment → returned organizational policy loop is established at the chosen recursion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: neither a static covenant/policy nor human approval over ordinary current work is sufficient to establish S5; no autonomous or parent S5 closure is inferred from enforcement. - Evidence: README.md, AGENTS.md.template, forge.skill.md and pre-tool-use.js were inspected for ultimate-policy/identity authority; the evidenced functions are current governance, safety and developer control. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/e7e949d44dbe4f440ff6ff73d380893b661b9351/assessments/cocoplus.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit e7e949d44dbe4f440ff6ff73d380893b661b9351\">e7e949d &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-21T15:07:59+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Xtra-Computing/MegaAgent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MegaAgent</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-21\">2026-09-21</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Xtra-Computing/MegaAgent/tree/c2e45ad99d8166db82b8f8516d2bcd722ad8540a\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision c2e45ad99d8166db82b8f8516d2bcd722ad8540a\">c2e45ad &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously execute a bounded project contribution through a model-driven local task/tool loop and produce shared artifacts, tests, integration or other project outcomes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task ambiguity, collaborator messages, current shared artifacts, execution/test results, local TODO/status, tool failures and changing subtask needs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose local tool actions, artifact changes, communication, testing/execution steps and whether to recruit subordinate capacity within assigned constraints. - Decision owner: each model-driven Agent operational unit. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-agent queue/thread, memory and relevant-history retrieval, generated role prompt, bounded model rounds, task/TODO files, first-party tools, shared workspace and external model transport. - Closure path: assigned/local task + collaborator/artifact state → agent model decision → tool/message/file action → returned execution/file/peer observation → subsequent model decision → local deliverable/result. - Boundary reachability: root main.py directly instantiates the generated CEO and workers through the first-party Agent class; recursive addagent uses the same runtime. No downstream agent loop has to be supplied to obtain model-driven operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic queue/tool/runtime code transports and enforces actions, but substantive local work, tool selection, content and follow-up are chosen by each running model agent from current evidence. - Evidence: main.py, agent.py, llm.py. - Basis: explicit + structural + observed bundled trace. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: not every generated process is automatically a separate viable recursion; S1 credits task-owning operational agent loops at the declared project level.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate dependency/readiness mismatch and conflicting concurrent mutations among distinct parallel agent S1 units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: one unit acting before a required upstream artifact is available; two units observing/writing different versions of a shared artifact; stale overwrites and merge conflicts in the common project workspace. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide how to adjust local timing/work after collaborator readiness feedback and how to reconcile/retry a shared-artifact update after current content/hash or merge-conflict evidence is returned. - Decision owner: the affected model-driven operational agents, distributed across the collaborating S1 units; the Git lock/base-hash/merge machinery detects and enforces collision constraints but does not choose the revised content/work response. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: generated collaborator identities, asynchronous talk, per-agent TODO/status, shared file visibility, readfile current commit hash, writefile lock/base-hash requirement, Git commit/merge and conflict response. - Closure path: inter-agent dependency or shared-file collision risk → peer readiness message and/or version-aware write attempt → current readiness/content/hash/conflict evidence reaches affected agent → model chooses wait/retry/reconcile/change work → subsequent S1 behavior uses the coordinated state. - Boundary reachability: talk, shared-file read/write and Git conflict handling are standard tools generated for every root-runtime agent; the shipped Grace trace demonstrates the dependency-feedback path without downstream wiring. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a lock alone would be deterministic S2 support only. A is credited because concrete dependency/collision evidence is returned into a model-owned next decision: agents choose to wait, notify collaborators, reread or revise the content/work response. - Evidence: agent.py, llm.py, utils.py, logs/Grace.log. - Basis: structural + observed bundled trace. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic messaging/delegation is not the basis; the credited witnesses are concrete dependency timing and shared mutable-artifact collision. - Distinct S1 units: concurrently running task-owning agents such as implementation, integration and testing workers. - Inter-S1 disturbance: downstream work can begin before upstream deliverables exist, and parallel agents can act on stale/conflicting versions of shared files. - Attenuating coordination relation: peer readiness/blocker exchange plus hash-aware serialized Git mutation with current-state/conflict feedback. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the receiving model agent waits/resumes/reprioritizes or rereads/reconciles/retries against the returned shared state. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: each credited mechanism is tied to a concrete cross-unit instability—dependency readiness or concurrent shared-artifact mutation—and changes the next operational behavior that would otherwise interfere.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the project-wide current portfolio of commitments, priorities, readiness, defects and completion/rework on behalf of the whole organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incomplete or blocked workers, changing dependency readiness, integration/testing findings, defects requiring reassignment, project-wide quiescence with unfinished work and apparent completion that may still fail whole-project requirements. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose current task allocation/reallocation, testing priorities, corrective owner, additional agent capacity and whether the project should terminate or another work cycle should begin. - Decision owner: the model-driven CEO agent (Bob in the shipped default organization). - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: worker talk reports, CEO subordinate map, shared project artifacts, per-agent TODO/status, global running/idle monitor, automatic wake-up of unfinished agents and final all-idle completion signal. - Closure path: current whole-project reports/artifacts/global completion state → CEO current-control judgment → talk/addagent/inspection/testing/reassignment decision → affected S1 unit changes work → new reports/artifacts/tests return into CEO control. - Boundary reachability: root main.py always creates the generated CEO, sends it the project-start allocation instruction, monitors every live agent/TODO, and returns apparent-completion state to that same CEO for terminate-versus-reassign judgment. The bundled Bob trace shows live priority and corrective routing in this standard path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the global monitor deterministically observes/wakes and supplies state, but it does not choose the organizational response. The CEO model chooses who should work on what next, which defect/priorities matter and whether to create another corrective cycle. - Evidence: main.py, agent.py, logs/Bob.log, logs/Grace.log. - Basis: structural + observed bundled trace. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: initial decomposition alone would not establish S3. The positive mapping rests on the later live worker/test feedback and CEO intervention/reassignment loop plus the whole-organization completion/rework closure. - Whole-system current view: worker progress/blocker/test messages, shared output artifacts and the root monitor&#39;s all-agent idle/TODO state provide the CEO with current project-level distinctions rather than a single-child result only. - Current-control decision scope: current ownership, priorities, testing focus, corrective reassignment, additional staffing and terminate-versus-rework decisions affecting multiple operational units and whole-project completion. - Current-control intervention: Bob redirects Dave after Grace identifies an AI defect, instructs Grace to continue/re-test and can recruit/message workers or restart work after the final completion check. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: talk/reassignment enters the relevant agent queue; corrected artifacts and new tester/integrator reports return through the same first-party organization. - Why this is S3-specific rather than decomposition / routing / static workflow: the credited witness occurs after initial decomposition and changes live commitments in response to current cross-project evidence and defects.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge ordinary implementation/integration claims by inspecting and executing produced artifacts, then return discrepancies into corrective project control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: latent functional defects or incomplete behavior that are not revealed by ordinary implementer progress/completion messages. - Decisive decision or feedback right: determine from direct artifact/runtime testing whether a produced behavior passes or exposes a concrete defect and publish that finding into project control. - Decision owner: the distinct model-driven testing agent in the generated organization (Grace in the shipped default run); CEO S3 owns the subsequent corrective allocation rather than the tester silently modifying the implementation claim. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tester role assignment, readfile, execpythonfile/interactive execution, test artifacts/reports, talk to CEO/integrator, shared workspace and CEO reassignment channel. - Closure path: implementer/integrator reports and produced artifacts → separate tester reads/executes/tests operational output → tester identifies discrepancy and reports evidence → CEO routes finding to responsible implementation S1 and orders follow-up/re-test → corrected operation is tested/reported again. - Boundary reachability: the standard root organization-generation path can and in the bundled default run does create a distinct tester; its runtime uses the same first-party tools/message channels and the committed logs show the full finding-to-correction handoff. No external evaluator is imported. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the testing judgment and finding are produced by a separate model agent using direct operational evidence. Deterministic execution/file tools expose reality but do not decide whether the observed behavior satisfies the claim. - Evidence: main.py, llm.py, logs/Grace.log, logs/Bob.log. - Basis: observed bundled trace + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic logs and the CEO&#39;s own routine project monitoring are not credited as S3. The positive witness is the separately assigned tester&#39;s direct artifact/runtime evidence and its corrective return. - Claim being audited: implementation/integration units report that delivered modules and assembled behavior are ready or functioning. - Ordinary reporting path: implementers/integrator send progress/readiness messages to collaborators/CEO and update shared deliverables/status. - Complementary access path: a distinct tester reads the actual produced files and executes targeted behavior/tests rather than accepting those status reports. - Independence boundary: Grace is separately generated/assigned as testing work, not the producer of the AI/integration implementation being challenged; her test observations are reported to the CEO rather than being generated by Dave&#39;s own production loop. - Who acts on findings: Grace reports the hard-AI four-in-a-row failure to Bob; Bob sends the finding to Dave for defensive-logic correction, directs Grace to continue testing, and the later organization requests re-testing/integration feedback. - Why this is S3-specific rather than ordinary review / QA / logging / evaluation: the evidence path materially bypasses ordinary implementer claims, directly probes runtime behavior, finds a contradiction and closes that finding into corrective operational work.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no externally and prospectively oriented adaptation loop that changes future MegaAgent organizational capability is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the runtime reacts strongly to current task complexity, current shared artifacts and current test outcomes, but no evidenced function models changing external/future conditions for later organizational adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for selecting and promoting a future capability adaptation. - Decision owner: none established for S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: dynamic agent generation, recursive addagent, memory/history retrieval, current-task testing and task-specific reorganization. - Closure path: current task/complexity → current roster/subtask adaptation → current project execution; no prospective environmental distinction → future adaptation options → selected/promoted later capability → present S3 conversation is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agents autonomously change current staffing and work in response to present operational variety, but Profile 0.2.3 explicitly separates current task planning/reaction from S4 future/environment intelligence. - Evidence: README.md, agent.py, config.py. - Basis: structural absence after prospective-adaptation review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: dynamically generating hundreds of agents demonstrates current variety amplification/scaling, not by itself future-oriented S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime identity/ultimate-policy decision loop is established at the MegaAgent project recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the user project goal, CEO name, model/provider choice, maximum rounds/subordinates and additional prompt constrain current operation, but no identity-level S3–S4 tension or ultimate-policy issue enters an authoritative runtime closure. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S5. - Decision owner: none established for S5 publication. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: config.initialprompt, additionalprompt, user-edited project goal/provider settings, static limits and CEO role generation. - Closure path: no identity/ultimate-policy issue → legitimate ultimate authority → authoritative decision → returned governance of subsequent operation path is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the CEO is an executive/control role for the current project, but executive naming and ordinary project completion authority are not S5. The user supplies initial purpose/configuration before operation; no parent runtime identity escalation path is shown. - Evidence: config.py, main.py, README.md. - Basis: structural absence after identity/policy review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a downstream deployment could wrap MegaAgent in human/institutional governance, but that would be a different system boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/2022cc46ef0c83cc6181ff994e9df4bb826f5d11/assessments/megaagent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 2022cc46ef0c83cc6181ff994e9df4bb826f5d11\">2022cc4 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-20T21:45:19+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/openags/auto-researcher\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OpenAGS</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-20\">2026-09-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/openags/auto-researcher/tree/9bb9dfc6d5e66ad92e3652c8a2ed250d62f8c11f\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 9bb9dfc6d5e66ad92e3652c8a2ed250d62f8c11f\">9bb9dfc &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: specialist research agents perform the operational scientific work: literature review, proposal development, experiments, manuscript production, and peer review/revision artifacts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: open-ended scientific questions, external literature, experiment implementation/execution, result interpretation, and manuscript construction. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next specialist reasoning/tool/file action needed to produce each role&#39;s research outcome within its SOUL/skill contract. - Decision owner: each first-party builtin OpenAGS Agent executing the corresponding specialist folder/role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SOUL and skill loading, tool engine, memory/session/history, filesystem workspaces, upstream/downstream paths, LiteLLM transport, timeouts, and orchestrator lifecycle. - Closure path: observations/tool results return to the specialist Agent loop; the agent updates role artifacts/status and those outputs become available to downstream agents/coordinator. - Boundary reachability: the repository documents a complete first-party builtin Agent path (Agent.loop) used by the research layer; S1 therefore does not depend on Claude Code/Codex/Cursor/Gemini owning the operational loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the first-party agent engine closes model decision → tool execution → observation → next decision inside the supported builtin mode. - Evidence: builtin-agent execution path, folder-as-agent model, default specialist workflow. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: alternate CLI-agent backends are not used to justify S1 ownership; the credited mode is the self-contained builtin OpenAGS agent engine.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material S2-specific regulation of a demonstrated inter-S1 conflict/oscillation is established at the declared project recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established as S2. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: upstream/downstream file contracts, fixed first-pass sequence, TASKS/STATUS files, agent dispatch, and shared project artifacts organize work but do not by themselves establish an inter-S1 interference witness. - Closure path: workflow outputs feed downstream specialists, but that sequencing/dependency path is not sufficient for S2. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: named multi-agent roles and communication through files are generic organization/hand-off primitives unless tied to a concrete conflict or oscillation between S1 units and an attenuation loop. - Evidence: file-based agent communication, coordinator first-pass sequence. - Basis: structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: workflow dependency management may prevent some practical clashes, but the required S2-specific disturbance/attenuation witness is not established in the reviewed evidence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: maintain a whole-project current view and decide which specialist work starts, waits, reruns, or is complete. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incomplete or failed stages, changing project status, downstream dependency readiness, review-identified weaknesses, and need to redirect current research effort. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next active specialist/task and, after the first pass, choose which research stages must rerun in response to current project evidence. - Decision owner: the root coordinator Agent in Auto Mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: specialist STATUS.md and TASKS.md, workflow/orchestrator process management, timeout/failure handling, fixed initial stage order, project files, and start/wait/all-complete action protocol. - Closure path: coordinator reads current statuses/review findings, emits startagent/wait/allcomplete/needshuman decisions and writes/assigns specialist tasks; the orchestrator launches or withholds subsequent work accordingly. - Boundary reachability: Auto Mode and the root coordinator are part of the default first-party project template and builtin workflow, not an external operator or development-only controller. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic orchestration executes start/stop/status mechanics, while the coordinator Agent is explicitly instructed to make pipeline decisions and decide what happens next from the whole project state. - Whole-system current view: all specialist status files, project-level memory/key decisions, current stage completion, and review outputs across the research project. - Current-control decision scope: assignment and ordering of current specialist work, whether to wait, whether the project is complete, and which stages to rerun after review. - Evidence: coordinator Auto Mode and role, workflow protocol. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: fixed first-pass sequencing is enforcement/support; S3 credit comes from the coordinator&#39;s evidenced discretionary current-control decisions, especially status-based dispatch and post-review rerun choice.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently review the manuscript/research claims using complementary access to manuscript, experiment results, literature notes, and external citation verification, then return actionable findings into project control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unsupported claims, weak methodology/experiments, citation hallucination or misrepresentation, missing baselines/limitations, and paper-level weaknesses not caught by ordinary production. - Decisive decision or feedback right: make peer-review judgments, classify major/minor concerns, score review criteria, issue a verdict, and prescribe a revision roadmap. - Decision owner: the distinct review Agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: dedicated review SOUL/folder, read access to manuscript/experiment/literature artifacts, web search for citation spot-checking, structured review format, and persisted review-report.md. - Closure path: review findings are written under review/reviews/; after the first pass the coordinator reads those weaknesses and decides which stages need to rerun, returning audit findings into changed subsequent operation. - Boundary reachability: the reviewer is a default specialist in the standard project template and a standard first-pass stage; its report is explicitly part of the coordinator&#39;s iteration mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the reviewer makes substantive adversarial/audit judgments rather than merely applying a deterministic syntax gate; the coordinator, not the reviewer, owns corrective project-control decisions. - Claim being audited: the manuscript&#39;s scientific claims, citations, experimental support, logic, completeness, and reproducibility. - Ordinary reporting path: manuscript output and specialist status/results flow normally into the project filesystem and coordinator. - Complementary access path: the reviewer reads the full manuscript plus experiment report plus literature review and independently web-searches sampled citations; it is instructed to adversarially probe alternative explanations/failure conditions/missing experiments. - Independence boundary: a separate review role/folder with its own SOUL and artifact is outside the manuscript-producing agent&#39;s ordinary production loop, while retaining direct access to underlying experiment/literature evidence. - Who acts on findings: the root coordinator reads review weaknesses and selects which stages to rerun; affected specialists then perform the corrective work. - Evidence: reviewer SOUL, coordinator iteration mode. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: model/provider independence is not claimed; organizational independence comes from the distinct review role, separate artifact, complementary evidence surfaces, and explicit findings-to-control return path.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: sense the external scientific landscape through the literature specialist and turn those distinctions into future-facing research proposals/experiment direction consumed by later S1 work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: risk of pursuing already-solved, poorly grounded, or scientifically irrelevant directions and missing externally established methods/results that should shape the project. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select and synthesize externally retrieved papers/field distinctions that should inform the project proposal and later research program. - Decision owner: the literature Agent, with downstream proposal/experiment agents autonomously using the returned research intelligence. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: paper-search skills/web access, literature notes/memory, fixed upstream paths, shared project filesystem, and coordinator sequencing. - Closure path: literature agent searches/reads external work and writes literature notes; the proposal agent explicitly reads ../literature/notes/ and memory.md; experiment/manuscript stages consume the proposal/literature outputs, changing subsequent project work. - Boundary reachability: literature is a default first-party specialist stage in the project template, and its artifacts are wired as upstream inputs to subsequent specialists in every supported runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the first-party literature Agent decides what external evidence is relevant and synthesizes it; file plumbing only returns that intelligence to current capability. - External distinction: current prior papers, methods, results, and gaps retrieved from outside the project. - Future / prospective distinction: which field gaps/constraints should shape the proposal and experiments the project has not yet executed. - Adaptation option generated: literature-grounded research/proposal directions and constraints for experiment design. - Path back into current capability / S3: persisted literature notes become explicit upstream inputs to proposal and later experiment/manuscript work under coordinator sequencing. - Evidence: folder/agent upstream-downstream architecture, research workflow skill. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: project memory and peer review alone are not used to justify S4; the credit rests on external scientific sensing and a closed return path into future project work.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy closure is established at the research-project recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established at S5 scope. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SOUL role definitions, user project brief, templates, skills, provider configuration, and needshuman escalation constrain operation but do not themselves establish ultimate-policy governance. - Closure path: no evidenced identity/policy issue → legitimate ultimate authority → authoritative decision → returned operation loop is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the coordinator manages current work but is not shown redefining the project&#39;s ultimate identity/policy; generic human input/escalation is not automatically parent-governed S5. - Evidence: coordinator SOUL, architecture. - Basis: structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a user can direct a project and the coordinator can request human input, but neither fact alone establishes S5 without a genuine ultimate-policy issue and authoritative return path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c9da28372ac35ff5d8bc9a74679a6987a5d451df/assessments/openags.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c9da28372ac35ff5d8bc9a74679a6987a5d451df\">c9da283 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-13T20:00:45+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/PawanOsman/OpenCursor\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OpenCursor</a><small>2022</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-13\">2026-10-13</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/PawanOsman/OpenCursor/tree/b4528f95ca5ab4cddf22bef3e19431fad00feb01\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision b4528f95ca5ab4cddf22bef3e19431fad00feb01\">b4528f9 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: directly interpret, inspect, edit and verify software against a human coding task. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown code and runtime behavior, incomplete edits, tool errors and feedback from repository/test inspection. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose file reads, edits, tool calls, retries, delegated S1 work and final response based on live evidence. - Decision owner: the model-driven coding agent in Agent mode and coding-worker subagents in supported Project/Multitask modes. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: VS Code tool bridge, file and shell effect adapters, permission/approval policies, session persistence and job display. - Closure path: agent choice → tool execution/permission result → actual file/shell observation → subsequent model decision or answer. - Boundary reachability: the packaged extension directly wires its agent loop and repository tools, no custom embedding host or optional external agent workflow is necessary. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the acting model leaves tool wrappers but no task-driven selection/repair; human approves permitted effects but does not choose every next coding action. - Evidence: README.md; loop.ts; index.ts; files.ts; approvalPolicy.ts - Basis: explicit and structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate shared-workspace change collisions and conflicting edits between independently acting delegated code-writing workers in the shipped coordinator/team modes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: at least two concurrently active coding children may attempt incompatible modifications or overwrite/revert another worker&#39;s output in their shared checkout. - Decisive decision or feedback right: lead model selects nonoverlapping assignments, sends corrective messages when a child flags conflict, and revises affected children rather than blindly accepting conflicting edits. - Decision owner: the lead agent/model in opt-in Project/Multitask configurations; child agents report overlaps; deterministic mailbox and a concurrency cap only execute the decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Task schema&#39;s &#39;assign separate files&#39; contract, built-in team protocol&#39;s non-overwrite/flag instruction, stable child IDs, collaborator status, message/follow-up controls. - Closure path: child conflict signal or current roster/result → lead&#39;s scope/plan correction → SendAgentMessage/FollowupAgent or new separate assignment → children&#39;s subsequent work; separation also happens prospectively when lead initially assigns distinct files. - Boundary reachability: built-in Project/Multitask prompt paths and child/messaging tools are wired in the standard extension, not derived from a user-created orchestration script. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: task-scope choice and conflict response are discretionary lead-model decisions; the active-child cap and status store do not own that interference-specific decision. - Evidence: loop.ts; schemas.ts; teams.ts; backgroundTasks.ts; collaborationState.ts - Basis: explicit mode instructions and structural code-wired feedback - Confidence: medium; a supported decision path, not proof that every conflict is detected or prevented - Caveats: children usually share a workspace; conversation worktrees are optional isolation and not automatic per-child reservations. Ordinary Agent mode does not establish this coordinated path on every turn. - Distinct S1 units: concurrent model/tool-running coding children started via the Task tool, each with its own history, task and executable edit tools. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overwriting/reverting another child&#39;s files or incompatible edits in the shared workspace, explicitly addressed by the shipped Team protocol and Task tool. - Attenuating coordination relation: lead chooses separate file assignments; built-in team members must adapt/flag cross-member conflicts, while root can steer/interrupt/reassign them. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: child reports and roster responses enter the lead turn; message/follow-up deliver revised instructions to named still-running or resumed children. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the shipped instructions target a concrete shared-file interference, not only transporting tasks; lead feedback can alter worker scopes after conflict.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the set of active delegated commitments across current Project/Multitask coding operations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple live coding children compete for bounded concurrent slots, priorities and dependencies while task results and interruptions change the feasible current plan. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect the complete conversation collaborator roster/status and choose which current worker gets each unit, is steered, followed up or interrupted; revise running commitments in response to results. - Decision owner: the opt-in Project/Multitask lead model; not the fixed max-four gate, job registry, event stream or human authorization engine. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ListAgents and stable IDs, activeAgents map, child status/result feedback, SendAgentMessage/FollowupAgent/InterruptAgent, root&#39;s intentionally disabled editing tools. - Closure path: whole-session roster/task reports → lead&#39;s portfolio-level assignment/intervention decision → launch/message/interrupt and changed child work → updated statuses/results. - Boundary reachability: the standard Project and Multitask modes explicitly put an LLM lead over all delegated coding tasks with model-facing operational management tools. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the lead model leaves a cap and worker status API but not a discretionary choice of how to reallocate current work; the supported lead modes are wider than one routine child tool call. - Evidence: loop.ts; schemas.ts; collaborationState.ts; backgroundTasks.ts; taskState.ts - Basis: structural and explicit first-party coordinator-mode instructions - Confidence: medium; session-scoped, not organizational or multi-deployment management - Caveats: not attributed to ordinary single-worker Agent mode or separate VS Code sessions. Does not mean that all child steps are autonomously centralized. - Whole-system current view: ListAgents exposes all retained conversation collaborators, live status/result and queued-message counts; background job registry tracks running/completed work. - Current-control decision scope: lead-mode model decides present unit assignment, bounded concurrency slot utilization, correction/interrupt, follow-up and completion of delegated commitments.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently inspect the implementing agent&#39;s code claims using a separate read-only code-reviewer context and raw repository/diff evidence, then return findings for control of repairs. - Disturbance / variety regulated: an implementer&#39;s self-report or ordinary integrated tool-status check may omit real defects, concurrency problems or unsafe edits. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a separate reviewer agent evaluates the actual diff and source context for defects; the implementing parent receives and decides follow-up repair or rejection. - Decision owner: the delegated reviewer model for the complementary judgment, acting only when commissioned, and the lead/implementer model for the downstream corrective choice. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: built-in read-only code-reviewer and team-code-reviewer roles, child independent history, file-reading/search tools, returned report and parent Task result. - Closure path: parent requests review → separate child reads current repository/diff and forms defect-first findings → report returns to parent history → implementing lead can fix, stop or ask for further check. - Boundary reachability: built-in code-reviewer Task type and built-in team-code-reviewer are available in the shipped extension and run through first-party child-model/tool execution; no user-authored reviewer is needed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the reviewer performs an independent direct-evidence judgment; parent runtime reports/forwards but does not supply the reviewer&#39;s verdict. Ordinary VerificationLedger is excluded from S3 credit. - Evidence: teams.ts; schemas.ts; loop.ts; files.ts; verification.ts - Basis: explicit built-in independent reviewer instructions and source-wired child execution - Confidence: medium-high for opt-in separate-context code review, not for default every-turn checks - Caveats: review and corrective response are conditional on the model/parent invoking the supported reviewer; no claim of Organization S3 governance or fully independent remote operator. - Claim being audited: whether implementing-agent code modifications are correct, safe and maintainable. - Ordinary reporting path: implementer tool outputs, final answer and VerificationLedger test-status summary. - Complementary access path: read-only separately instantiated reviewer model with direct code/diff/repository access and explicit defect-first prompt. - Independence boundary: different child model context/history/role and direct artifacts rather than merely parsing the implementer&#39;s natural-language self-report. - Who acts on findings: calling lead/implementer receives findings through Task result and can select corrective work.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no separate external-and-prospective organizational capability-adaptation loop is established in the distributed extension. - Disturbance / variety regulated: external documentation, model/tool changes and potential future feature requirements. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no shipped S4-specific adaptive decision/feedback owner identified. - Decision owner: model for present coding and documentation retrieval; user or extension settings for selecting models/skills; neither closes future adaptation of the harness organization. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: docs discovery/indexing, semantic repository retrieval, reusable personas/instructions, task memory and provider choice. - Closure path: information returns to task context, but no first-party future capability strategy is selected and returned to ongoing operational capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: document discovery and planning for current coding work is not a prospective S4 organizational judgment. - Evidence: docsPlanner.ts; loop.ts; README.md; taskState.ts - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity/ultimate-policy constitutional decision path with binding return is supplied for the coding-session organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ultimate purpose or policy conflicts as distinct from one tool&#39;s allowed/denied action. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party S5 decisive authority; ordinary permission review authorizes individual actions. - Decision owner: user/host settings and project operators outside the assessed system-in-focus; no evidenced internal/parent constitutional decision chain. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: approval policy, read-only modes, tool restrictions, workspace instructions and team role prompts. - Closure path: action approvals return allow/deny to ordinary task execution; no identity-level matter → parent ultimate decision → changed governing operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: tool policy is a safety constraint, not the identity-policy function; an operator&#39;s wider development organization is not imported into this one-session boundary. - Evidence: approvalPolicy.ts; loop.ts; schemas.ts; README.md; teams.ts - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/37c1aace4e11d50dbdda13b256534152d0169eea/assessments/opencursor.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 37c1aace4e11d50dbdda13b256534152d0169eea\">37c1aac &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-31T23:56:39+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/dohwi/pipaya\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pipaya</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-31\">2026-10-31</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/dohwi/pipaya/tree/dd5cdfd560c1499301a1f2fe4c07f9173a12d53a\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision dd5cdfd560c1499301a1f2fe4c07f9173a12d53a\">dd5cdfd &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Persistent model-backed workspace sessions perform coding/investigation tasks and can use disposable helpers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User requests, file/command results, provider faults, context pressure and delegated observations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose workspace tools, follow-up actions and final answers; choose whether to delegate or create a persistent peer. - Decision owner: The configured session agent, composed by the first-party SessionManager around Pi Agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Pi inference/loop dependency, first-party tools, SQLite transcript/context state, queues, compaction, retry and limits. - Closure path: Prompt → constructed Agent with owned tool definitions → selected calls → observations/persistence → further turns and final response. - Boundary reachability: ensureLive creates Agent with the session&#39;s actual model, history, prompt and first-party tools. The packaged server and desktop-bundled server wire this composition directly. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The first-party product owns the concrete executable composition; Pi SDK is a dependency and does not donate uninstantiated higher functions. Without the session actor, server APIs and gates do not choose project work. - Evidence: [Session construction and helper execution][R1], [tools][R2], [server distribution][R3], [manifest][R4]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: No provider-backed E2E run or Pi-wide capability claim is made.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: The supplied peer protocol attenuates reciprocal reply/notification oscillation between independently running sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two peer sessions can repeatedly reply to one another or cause completion notifications to trigger further replies. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The receiving agent decides whether a peer response is needed and whether to end that exchange; the sender chooses explicit reply/notification requests. - Decision owner: The participating session agents own semantic response necessity; runtime-authored notification and send bounds constrain the exchange. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: peerMessage marks other-session origin and says to reply only if needed; completion watches are consumed once, reports say no reply needed, and per-run send overflow returns stop/report feedback. - Closure path: Peer message or one-shot completion report → recipient agent sees explicit reply/termination semantics → chooses further peersend only when needed, or stops; cap refusal feeds back to the sender&#39;s next action. - Boundary reachability: Peer tools are automatically included in normal session tool plans unless the operator disables them. PeerService wraps actual delivered messages and sends report feedback into prompt/followup paths of ordinary agents. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The narrow credited discretion is agent choice over necessary replies under a first-party anti-oscillation protocol. The generic mailbox, layout groups and delegation are not themselves the S2 witness, and the counter is not an autonomous owner. - Evidence: [Peer templates, send counter and one-shot watchers][R5], [tool descriptions][R2], [always-reachable peer tool wiring][R1], [tool plan][R6]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: This is bounded structural attenuation, not proof that arbitrary agent conversations terminate. The counter resets each run and cannot alone rule out cross-run ping-pong or shared-file collisions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: A session agent can regulate current commitments across peer sessions, and the local human operator has a distinct supplied supervisory mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Peers are stuck, working on the wrong course, waiting on questions, or consuming the wrong model/active-session capacity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Inspect all peer state, steer/abort work, create/close/resume sessions, withdraw queued work, change model/effort and answer blocked questions; in parent mode the operator makes those current-control choices through the server/client API. - Decision owner: Base mode: a model-backed session acting as peer supervisor. Parent mode: the local server operator using the administrator-authority client/API. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: peerlist/read/current watcher notifications, peersend steer/followup, peerabort/manage/create/answer, persisted session state and authenticated REST/WebSocket interfaces. - Closure path: Current peer view/report → agent or operator selects intervention → SessionManager applies queue/run/model/lifecycle/question changes → subsequent peer execution follows the changed current commitment. - Boundary reachability: Every normal session receives the first-party peer tools, wired to SessionManager host methods, unless disabled. The client/API exposes server-wide activity/session/status/usage and actual abort/message/patch/question endpoints for its operator. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the supervisory actor leaves APIs, messages and limits but no discretionary selection of whom to stop, steer, resume or reconfigure. Parent control is evidenced separately from generic askuser approval. - Evidence: [Peer state/intervention methods][R5], [specific tool semantics][R2], [SessionManager host wiring][R1], [authenticated operator routes][R7], [server API contract][R3]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Authority is broad among sessions of one trusted server; this is neither a global organizational claim nor evidence that every run uses a supervisor.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No shipped audit-specific independent challenge-and-corrective-control loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A peer or helper may make an incorrect completion claim. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No supplied audit role/verdict/return protocol decides that claim independently. - Decision owner: Ordinary producing sessions report their work. Helper role files and extension logic are user supplied. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: peerread can inspect ordinary transcript/tool snippets; workspace read/grep and generic helpers are available; event/context/read tracing preserve evidence. - Closure path: Peer/helper output returns as ordinary tool text or messages, without an audit-specific independently controlled adjudication and corrective path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A fresh helper context and raw file tools can be used to build a reviewer, but the shipped delegate interface itself is generic operational delegation. No bundled reviewer is credited from an external marketplace. - Evidence: [Helper definition/execution][R8], [session delegate][R1], [peer read][R5], [extension boundary][R9]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Users can create a material complementary audit application; it needs its own independent owner, evidence-access and corrective-return proof.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No external-and-prospective adaptation loop is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Provider configuration, new skills/extensions, changing instructions and available upstream releases. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No first-party intelligence path develops future-relevant options and negotiates a return into current capability. - Decision owner: Users configure endpoints/extensions/skills and request updates; sessions perform requested present tasks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AGENTS.md/skill refresh, compaction, provider settings, extension APIs and release-tag updater. - Closure path: Settings apply next run; updater installs the latest release after operator request and running-session checks. No prospective environment model/adaptation-option decision loop is wired. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A latest-tag inequality check and package restart are maintenance, not external/future adaptation judgment. General capability extension and compaction are not S4; no parent adaptation mode is inferred from update consent. - Evidence: [Source update policy][R10], [live context/skills][R11], [SessionManager][R1], [extension skill boundary][R9]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: The assessment does not claim that users cannot organize an adaptive workflow on this product.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No identity/ultimate-policy adjudication-and-return loop is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Session instructions, permissions, enabled skills/tools and broad server administrator control. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No identity-level issue is routed to ultimate authority and returned as an authoritative system policy decision. - Decision owner: Users author prompts/settings; trusted extensions run with server privileges; agents can change bounded peer metadata/current work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Authentication token, frozen initial tool/base-prompt snapshot, live AGENTS/skill settings, extension approval and per-tool enforcement. - Closure path: Settings constrain subsequent calls, and askuser/peeranswer returns ordinary question answers. Those are not a supplied identity-policy adjudication protocol. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A field named purpose, a user answer and administrator control are not S5 without a function-specific ultimate-policy issue and governing return. - Evidence: [Context and instruction boundary][R11], [session controls][R1], [question lifecycle][R12], [operator API][R7], [peer metadata][R5]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: The S3 parent mode is not reused as S5 evidence; external extension policy does not enter the first-party default boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/af57467e7adc84c630a0ffc6bb9cfb46e586a39f/assessments/pi/pipaya.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit af57467e7adc84c630a0ffc6bb9cfb46e586a39f\">af57467 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-27T21:17:31+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/rokartur/agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">agent (rokartur)</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-27\">2026-10-27</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/rokartur/agent/tree/f02a473edd8a08368876e45a641f8ed8c2a4c85d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision f02a473edd8a08368876e45a641f8ed8c2a4c85d\">f02a473 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: coding transformation by root and child agent loops - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown source, tool failures, changing files and delegated findings - Decisive decision or feedback right: select tool/action/arguments and revise the approach from results - Decision owner: root/child models within their assigned scopes - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider adapters, permissions, tool dispatcher, JSONL persistence, snapshots and channels - Closure path: model tool choice → execution result → next model input; child answer/follow-up → root continuation - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the model leaves execution support but no discretionary solution. - Evidence: source/src/agent/mod.rs#L539-L670, source/src/agent/subagent.rs#L188-L215, source/src/agent/subagent.rs#L263-L323 - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: Pinned source establishes a supported path, not observed effectiveness or universal deployment. - Boundary reachability: Agent::runturns is called by supported CLI/server modes; child launch uses the current installed executable.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: prevent stale writes from overwriting another operational writer&#39;s changes - Disturbance / variety regulated: root/parallel children can act on the same cwd after reading different file versions - Decisive decision or feedback right: reject stale anchored edits and return fresh-read feedback; broader coordination discretion still needs composition - Decision owner: first-party anchor controller enforces the rule; no separately assigned autonomous cross-writer coordination owner - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: session-specific anchors, reread/sync before edit, model-visible error and common child cwd - Closure path: another writer changes file → stale anchor rejected → caller receives ESTALEANCHOR and must reread before a valid subsequent edit - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A concrete collision-specific path exists; deterministic rejection does not itself own autonomous coordination judgment. C credits this narrow supplied path, not the ability to program any coordinator. - Evidence: source/src/tools/hashline.rs#L308-L329, source/src/tools/hashline.rs#L654-L659, source/src/agent/mod.rs#L975-L1004, source/src/agent/mod.rs#L640-L666 - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: Pinned source establishes a supported path, not observed effectiveness or universal deployment. - Boundary reachability: Hashline-backed tools are installed for the coding agents and child launch defaults to the root cwd. - Distinct S1 units: root coding agent and parallel child coding agents. - Inter-S1 disturbance: stale read-modify-write on shared files after a different writer edits them. - Attenuating coordination relation: anchor ownership plus reread/sync rejects stale range rather than silently overwriting. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: caller receives explicit fresh-read instruction in tool result, then chooses its next action. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the credited path is version-sensitive writer-conflict rejection, not child spawning, work ordering or a mailbox.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: current cohesion and accountability of the bounded child task team - Disturbance / variety regulated: different child states, failed/superseded work and model/effort commitments - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect team roster/results, change active child scope, stop and resume commitments, select model/effort for replacement work - Decision owner: root model for its current team - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ListAgents, SendMessage, TaskStop, subagent state and follow-up queues; static concurrency cap is enforcement - Closure path: team status/result → root current judgment → steering/stop/resume/new scoped resource choice → changed child operations → later state/result - Why this is / is not agent-owned: This is narrower than global fleet governance and exceeds initial decomposition: a running child can be steered, stopped and resumed after accountability feedback. - Evidence: source/src/agent/mod.rs#L892-L1029, source/src/agent/subagent.rs#L92-L108, source/src/tools/tools.json, source/src/agent/swarm-prompt.md#L18-L35 - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: Task-team scope only. Generic human plan approval, session interruption and permission-mode edits are not credited as a separate parent S3 mode. - Boundary reachability: Root&#39;s shipped tool set exposes status and intervention; follow-ups enter its continuing run loop. No custom control-plane code is required. - Whole-system current view: root&#39;s roster of its child tasks/status plus returned answers, within the task-team recursion. - Current-control decision scope: ongoing team commitments, task scope, model/effort resources and intervention; not provider infrastructure or unrelated sessions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: challenge delegated outcome claims with complementary primary-artifact access - Disturbance / variety regulated: a child answer can be mistaken despite appearing complete - Decisive decision or feedback right: independently open cited source or rerun the decisive command before relying on the child&#39;s claim - Decision owner: root agent as a decision holder distinct from the child producer - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: built-in swarm guidance embedded in Agent tool description, root Read/Bash, returned child claim and root continuation - Closure path: child answer → root selects checks on depended-on claims → raw artifact/command evidence → root revises or withholds next work and can steer/resume the child - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The shipped instruction explicitly treats the answer as a claim and names an alternate evidence path; judgment resides with the root, not a deterministic hook. This does not rely on naming an arbitrary spawned child &#39;auditor&#39;. - Evidence: source/src/agent/swarm-prompt.md#L18-L27, source/src/tools/mod.rs#L38-L43, source/src/agent/mod.rs#L1022-L1029, source/src/agent/mod.rs#L892-L920 - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: Prompt-guided supported audit mode, not evidence that every run performs it. Auto permission reviewer and deep-research&#39;s mandatory verify phase are not used to establish complementary audit. - Boundary reachability: The default bundled swarm prompt is injected into the root&#39;s Agent tool description; root already has source/command tools and consumes child results in supported modes. - Claim being audited: child&#39;s completion/finding claims on which the root&#39;s next step depends. - Ordinary reporting path: child final answer or queued background follow-up. - Complementary access path: root independently reads cited path:line or reruns decisive command against workspace reality. - Independence boundary: root context/judgment is separate from the child production context; model provider may be shared, so this is workflow/claim independence, not model-diversity assurance. - Who acts on findings: root changes its own next step or sends corrective child instructions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: outside-and-then adaptation of the harness/team capability - Disturbance / variety regulated: Web research, long-term goals and self-update are not by themselves prospective system adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No function-specific decisive path established. - Decision owner: None established for this function in the assessed mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: bundled deep-research/workflows, goal/todo/cron, model routing, compaction, update and skills - Closure path: No qualifying function-specific return into operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Deep-research produces the requested research report; updates follow published versions. No shipped loop turns external prospective distinctions into adaptation options and a current-capability regulatory return; parent adaptation was not established. - Evidence: source/src/agent/deep-research.js, source/src/update.rs, source/src/tools/goal.rs, source/src/agent/workflow.rs - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: identity / ultimate-policy closure - Disturbance / variety regulated: Ask/Auto/FullAccess permissions, trust and prompt policy bound ordinary tool actions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No function-specific decisive path established. - Decision owner: None established for this function in the assessed mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: permission reviewer, trusted project instructions, plan approval, settings, root swarm constraints and session UI - Closure path: No qualifying function-specific return into operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The review judges an individual proposed action against the existing request; it neither resolves identity-policy tension nor constructs an authoritative policy-return loop. Mutable prompts and contributor governance do not close S5 for the deployed task team. - Evidence: source/src/agent/permissions.rs#L178-L229, source/src/agent/mod.rs#L1106-L1141, source/src/agent/prompt.rs - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/28430b0a3ee80e366fca884d46873921a7deb469/assessments/ag/agent-rokartur.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 28430b0a3ee80e366fca884d46873921a7deb469\">28430b0 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-26T20:37:16+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/dtadpole/heddle\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Heddle</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-26\">2026-10-26</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/dtadpole/heddle/tree/6ae7e8dd80931c69946b3f1d75d523c77a99252d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 6ae7e8dd80931c69946b3f1d75d523c77a99252d\">6ae7e8d &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: produce requested workspace/task results through an iterative model/tool process. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown files, tool observations, permission refusals, new user input and model/context limitations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select the next tool, arguments, follow-up investigation or final answer in light of returned observations. - Decision owner: the model-driven root/teammate agent running runTurn; the operator supplies its objective and permitted environment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Cordis loader, model adapter, context preparation/compaction, tool registry, sandbox, approvals, round limit and append-only session log. - Closure path: agent tool choice → tool output/refusal persisted in session → context prepared for the next model round → revised agent choice or completed result. - Boundary reachability: normal heddle CLI/Web modes use runTurn; the optional Team plugin also calls the same loop for teammate turns. No outside coding-agent CLI is needed for the witness. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the model&#39;s ongoing decisions, deterministic transport and tool handling cannot choose the task-specific next action. - Evidence: loop, Team plugin, profile. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: source evidence establishes an executable decision/action path, not model task quality or reliability; no live provider run was made.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate work-claim and ordering conflicts between distinct teammate S1 units on one team board. - Distinct S1 units: Lead and autonomous teammate turns, or two independently running teammates whose work shares the team task board and filesystem. - Inter-S1 disturbance: competing claims of one pending task, a stale task update after another teammate&#39;s change, or an attempt to start work before a dependent task is complete. Concurrent write-scope overlap is also surfaced as a warning, not a hard filesystem lock. - Attenuating coordination relation: version-checked claim/update, owner enforcement, dependency readiness, task views with scope-overlap warnings and direct teammate messaging. Agents can choose available work or revise task dependencies/claims after reading the resulting state. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: stale or occupied claims are refused with explicit error codes; the agent receives those tool results, can re-read the board and select a different claim or coordinate with another member before acting. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: compare-and-set revisions and ownership/readiness constraints explicitly address collisions among competing task actors; the returned conflict state changes which task work can begin. The mailbox alone would not suffice. - Disturbance / variety regulated: conflicting claims, out-of-date plans, blocked dependencies and overlapping teammate work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select/revise task ownership and dependency handling in response to live conflict signals. - Decision owner: Team-mode agents acting through the model-facing task tools; fixed CAS and access checks only enforce their attempts. The Lead also has the supervisory reassign right separately considered under S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: durable team/task event fold, revision comparison, ownership/lead checks, dependency DAG validation, TEAMTASKSTALE/TEAMTASKBLOCKED refusals and notification/mail transport. - Closure path: multiple teammates read shared work → agent proposes claim/plan → service accepts or refuses based on current conflict/dependency state → result enters agent&#39;s next model round → agent adjusts work selection or message/claim; accepted updates become the next shared task state. - Boundary reachability: the shipped team plugin registers task tools in heddle.patch.yml and wires them to the actual team session log; no developer-only verifier or external coordinator is borrowed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agents retain choice over which eligible tasks to claim, which conflicts to resolve by messaging and how to revise dependencies; removing their choices leaves only fixed admission guards, not a complete discretionary coordination response. This is a supported optional mode, not a claim that every single-agent run has S2. - Evidence: task state and CAS, model-facing task tools, Team service, policy note. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: write scopes are advisory; source supplies the decision and feedback channel, not an observed conflict-resolution quality result. An operator may direct a team, but no S2 parent notation is used.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: maintain whole-team current commitments and intervene across multiple ongoing task cells. - Whole-system current view: model-facing listagents and teamtasklist expose all members, their live/durable states, task readiness, owners, revisions and dependency blockers. - Current-control decision scope: the Lead can create and revise shared tasks/dependencies, reassign a teammate&#39;s claimed work, reopen work, stop an active teammate turn and send corrective instructions; ordinary teammates cannot reassign someone else&#39;s owned task. - Disturbance / variety regulated: an overloaded, blocked, stale or misassigned current team whose shared commitments cannot be resolved within one task cell. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose a whole-team assignment/priority/intervention based on the current board and member statuses, including interruption or reallocation. - Decision owner: the model-driven Team Lead in the first-party Team mode. Lead-only role checks and cancellation machinery enforce its chosen action; they do not choose the intervention. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: full team state fold, task ownership and revision gates, requireLead, turn registry and abort signal, serialized message delivery. - Closure path: Lead obtains team-wide status → decides to reassign/reopen/stop/message → Team service updates authoritative task/member operation or aborts a turn → affected teammate receives changed work/steered instruction and subsequent operation follows it. - Boundary reachability: first-party Team plugin registers listagents, teamtasklist, teamtaskupdate, sendmessage and interruptagent, with Lead authority and live turns support wired in the supplied profile. It does not depend on private contributor orchestration. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the Lead must select which live commitments to change; the runtime merely rejects illegal states and carries the selected change. A team-less profile has no S3 claim from this mechanism. - Evidence: Team service, task intervention rules, Team tools, profile. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: no claim of effective real-world team governance or independent current-performance metrics; hard limits and goal-round continuation alone would not establish S3. Operator instructions over ordinary tasks do not separately prove an S3 parent mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material first-party complementary independent audit-return path established for the installed team/product at this boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a teammate&#39;s potentially incorrect completion report or misleading task status. - Decisive decision or feedback right: an independently controlled judgment against direct operational evidence and subsequent corrective feedback is not a shipped Team-mode control loop. - Decision owner: the development-side verifier owners govern the separate project-checking process; runtime Lead/teammates can inspect and review but do not instantiate an independently governed complementary auditor by default. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: session/event logs, task status, developer verifiers, offline unit tests and review collection scripts. - Closure path: team task completion/status may inform routine Lead decisions; no separately controlled audit-of-reporting decision and return into Team control is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a verifier for Heddle&#39;s own implementation and ordinary self-reported task completion are not independent inspections of teammates&#39; operating reality at this recursion. - Evidence: Team service, verifier boundary, review collection. - Basis: structural, bounded negative. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: a user can independently instruct a teammate to inspect raw artifacts, but general tool expressiveness does not establish the function-specific independence and returned control in the shipped operating mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material prospective external-environment sensing, option development and return into installed team capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: future model/tool/environment changes rather than current task context or failure recovery. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose adaptation of the installation&#39;s future capability based on externally derived options; no such shipped decision path is identified. - Decision owner: operator/developer can update plugins and profiles outside the running organizational loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: persistent session/context, context compaction, goal continuation, endpoint selection and dynamic Cordis plugin loading. - Closure path: goals continue present work and context summaries feed the next task round; neither supplies prospective external intelligence with adopted strategic adaptation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: context and goal maintenance are within-task operations; plugin extensibility is not itself an adaptation regulator. - Evidence: goal driver, context composition, boot/configuration. - Basis: structural, bounded negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: custom plugins or an operator&#39;s separate adaptation program would require a new declared boundary and evidence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material installed-instance identity or ultimate-policy resolution loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: potential identity-level tensions about purpose or ultimate permissible operation, distinct from an ordinary tool authorization. - Decisive decision or feedback right: legitimate ultimate-policy judgment with an authoritative return to subsequent operation; not supplied by Heddle&#39;s approval dialogs. - Decision owner: operator selects standing permissions and profiles; project maintainer owns the separate product-development goal, not a shipped identity-level governance loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: allow/deny/ask tool permissions, per-call approval, sandbox modes, configuration file and team-role rules. - Closure path: human approval or denial returns to one tool call; it does not carry an identity/ultimate-policy issue to a legitimate parent and return a durable policy resolution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model cannot revise ultimate identity through a first-party policy-authority path; operator&#39;s ordinary edit/approval is not automatically S5=P. - Evidence: approval service, sandbox policy, development goal. - Basis: structural, bounded negative. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: an operator&#39;s real organization can have S5 outside this local installed harness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/cdef6102b9bb2fd020571ea0486c2be26ad22aec/assessments/dt/dtadpole-heddle.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit cdef6102b9bb2fd020571ea0486c2be26ad22aec\">cdef610 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-24T18:56:35+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/DMRLZZ/DaveCode\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">DaveCode</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-24\">2026-10-24</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/DMRLZZ/DaveCode/tree/271910253a005845174c2410dc15675877a9883e\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 271910253a005845174c2410dc15675877a9883e\">2719102 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute user-directed coding and goal/artifact work through model-owned next-action decisions and actual workspace/output feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: varying repository state, implementation alternatives, task dependencies, real tool outputs, failed quality gates, incomplete context, provider constraints and uncompleted goal artifacts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose which first-party coding tools to invoke and what code/action to attempt next; for artifact roles, generate a task-specific output from received objective and dependency evidence. - Decision owner: the selected model-backed BuiltinExecutor actor and goal role agents in supported first-party mode. The runner selects/validates task transitions deterministically and does not inherit the model&#39;s coding discretion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Router, quota/budget, WorkspaceTools, token/iteration ceilings, Git branch operations, task context, retry/repair policy, goal store and acceptance validators. - Closure path: current task/context → model requests repository tools → WorkspaceTools executes → exact tool result enters same conversation → next coding decision; goal artifact calls persist their output and return it to the subsequent goal review/task selection. - Boundary reachability: createRunner constructs the shipped built-in executor; wireProject exposes it through CLI/server; GoalOrchestrator.runCodeTask invokes the first-party runner while artifact work calls router.complete with first-party role prompts. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model-backed code/role actor leaves graph selection, router/failover and verification machinery, but no discretionary construction of task-specific code edits or artifacts from evidence. - Evidence: executor.ts; runner.ts; orchestrator.ts; runtime.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for shipped source construction; no provider-backed outcomes measured. - Caveats: only the native builtin composition supplies this positive S1 path. External CLI executor modes may have their own decision owner outside DaveCode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate competing operations&#39; shared-worktree mutation conflict between the first-party standalone autonomous runner and first-party goal operation that invokes code tasks on the same project. - Disturbance / variety regulated: both top-level execution modes check out Git branches and commit in one writable workspace; simultaneous use would race on branch/index/worktree state. - Distinct S1 units: the standalone AutonomousRunner coding unit and the goal-directed role/code operational composition exposed as a separate first-party GoalService. They are distinct runnable operational entry points to one repository, not two model calls within the same tool step. - Inter-S1 disturbance: competing top-level runner and goal ownership of one Git worktree can cause Git checkout/commit interference. workspace-lock.ts explicitly identifies this conflict. - Attenuating coordination relation: wireProject instantiates one shared WorkspaceLock; the runner acquires it as owner runner, goal mode holds it as goal, a competing owner receives busy, and nested goal-owned code runner uses reentrant goal ownership. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: rejected launch returns RunnerError(&#39;busy&#39;)/GoalError(&#39;busy&#39;) to the parent CLI/API caller, preventing conflicting execution; subsequent initiation must occur after the holder releases the lock. A model-owned cross-unit rescheduler is not instantiated. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: this is not a DAG edge or role routing: a first-party lock is explicitly tied to a real shared-Git mutation disturbance between two separately startable coding/goal operating units and changes their admission behaviour. - Decisive decision or feedback right: grant or refuse exclusive entry to the shared writable-workspace operating boundary and report the conflict for later caller scheduling. - Decision owner: deterministic WorkspaceLock enforces exclusivity according to fixed rules; a developer/operator would have to compose an autonomous policy for what postponed work to start next. The shipped S2-specific feedback path supports constructor state C, not autonomous A. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ownership-counting WorkspaceLock; shared wireProject runtime wiring; runner acquireLock and GoalService.start; goal RunnerCodeTasks reentrant owner. - Closure path: goal/runner start proposal → shared lock grants or rejects → only one writer progresses; rejection returns busy to caller and later attempts may proceed after release. Autonomous adaptive cross-unit scheduling remains to be composed. - Boundary reachability: the published CLI project wiring always shares the lock between the first-party goal and standalone runner for that project; this does not depend on an unshipped plugin. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the conflict-specific first-party lock enforces a fixed partition and returns a decision, but a coding model does not autonomously decide who waits, retries or reallocates operations after a collision. - Evidence: workspace-lock.ts; goal/service.ts; goal/code-tasks.ts; cli/runtime.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium; the claim is limited to exclusive shared-worktree admission, not rich semantic task coordination. - Caveats: lock is in-process and does not prove cross-process or distributed exclusion. Alternative top-level execution modes may be seen as exclusive rather than co-active S1s; this is the principal semantic boundary to review before canonical admission.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: regulate the present whole-goal task commitment population through reviewer-informed retries/new tasks and operator-managed task-graph priorities/constraints. - Disturbance / variety regulated: some goal tasks fail or become blocked, artifact/code outcomes vary, budget/task/round limits constrain current work and changed task priorities may require revised commitment selection. - Whole-system current view: goal reviewer receives every task&#39;s status, role, attempt count, title, notes and bounded actual artifact excerpt, plus budget context and current round. The CLI tasks listing and API expose the shared project task graph to operators. - Current-control decision scope: the goal reviewer decides when to retry failed work, which additional tasks enter the next round, and whether the bounded entire goal is complete. In operator mode, current project task priority/dependency/acceptance edits and status changes alter which pending commitment nextTask selects in later runner iterations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: revise current task commitments from whole-goal evidence and return the decision to task execution, or let legitimate project operator do so through task-graph control. - Decision owner: goal reviewer model actor for the autonomous A mode; project operator using first-party task list/edit/status API for the distinct P mode. Fixed task/round/token limits enforce bounds but do not own the discretionary choice. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: GoalOrchestrator.review, applyRetries/normalizeTasks, persisted GoalStore, task graph validation, nextTask, CLI/HTTP routes and fixed budgets/workspace locks. - Closure path: goal outcome/status/artifact excerpts → reviewer model produces retry/new task/finish judgment → orchestrator alters tasks and executes the next round. Separately, operator reads task graph → changes priority/dependencies/status → persisted project graph → next runner selection consumes the revised commitments. - Boundary reachability: first-party GoalService is constructed by createGoalService and wired into the published CLI/runtime; task listing/edit endpoints and runner graph reads are part of the same installed project mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the goal reviewer the deterministic DAG/limit machinery can walk given tasks but cannot make evidence-driven judgments to revise the current goal task portfolio. In operator mode the human makes those current-control decisions; the API only stores/enforces them. - Evidence: goal/orchestrator.ts; goal/service.ts; cli/commands/tasks.ts; server/routes/tasks.ts; brain/graph.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high for bounded goal-level S3; the operator mode is project-current-control only, not a claim of global DaveCode governance. - Caveats: a standalone runner&#39;s deterministic priority choice and stop button alone are not the autonomous S3 basis. The positive autonomous mode rests on the goal reviewer receiving whole-goal status and revising current commitments, not merely task decomposition.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: supply an optional independent acceptance judgment over actual staged code changes beyond the production actor&#39;s summary and configured deterministic check results, with corrective return. - Disturbance / variety regulated: coding actor may self-report completion despite a patch failing the task&#39;s acceptance criteria even when configured lints/tests pass. - Claim being audited: that the implementation diff fulfills each task acceptance criterion and can be accepted/merged. - Ordinary reporting path: built-in executor returns its summary and the Validator runs configured checks; without an independent configured judge, NoneJudge always passes after the checks. - Complementary access path: when runner.judge.kind=&#39;jev&#39; the first-party runner obtains actual staged Git diff/stat plus test validation, passes them and task acceptance to dedicated TypeSafe Jev via JevJudge, and receives a separate calibrated acceptance verdict. The alternative llm judge exposes the same inputs but its routing independence is not assumed. - Independence boundary: the Jev decision is made by an external dedicated evaluator on fresh raw Git diff/validation evidence with a separate model/service from the coding actor&#39;s implementation session, not by replaying the implementer&#39;s own success message. Source-level evidence supports the path; no live independence/accuracy experiment was run. - Who acts on findings: AutonomousRunner converts a failed judge verdict into a failure report for the subsequent implementation repair cycle, or marks the task FAILED when the configured retry limit is reached. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judge whether the actual change satisfies acceptance criteria independently of the coding actor&#39;s conclusion; determine acceptance or corrective retry. - Decision owner: the optional dedicated TypeSafe Jev evaluator model in the first-party jev supported mode; DaveCode&#39;s schema parser, confidence threshold, and runner enforce/transport its judgment without owning the evaluator&#39;s discretionary assessment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: createJudge, acceptance inputs/diff capture, score threshold, zod response validation, validator, repair-pass state machine and bounded failure handling. - Closure path: candidate diff/test results → independent Jev assessment → pass reaches delivery or reject is incorporated into failure text → built-in coding agent sees report and repairs → fresh diff is rejudged. - Boundary reachability: createRunner selects createJudge(cfg.judge, ...) from first-party runner config, which permits jev; runTask invokes it after successful deterministic validation as part of installed CLI/goal-driven coding operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the separate evaluator owns the acceptance judgment, while the runner and threshold gate enforce the returned verdict; it is not ordinary self-review by the production actor in this supported mode. - Evidence: autonomous/judge.ts; autonomous/create.ts; autonomous/runner.ts; config/schema.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium; optional independent judgment/return is clearly wired, but practical audit quality was not tested. - Caveats: default judge.kind=&#39;none&#39; has no independent audit; an llm judge on the same model route cannot be assumed independent. The A state credits the explicit first-party opt-in dedicated Jev mode only and does not imply all deployments instantiate it.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party external-and-prospective organizational capability adaptation loop established within the supported project runtime. - Disturbance / variety regulated: quota exhaustion, model availability and current-task failures are detected; internal replanning/repair and operational failover respond to present conditions, not outside-and-then organizational capability evolution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no separate prospectively sourced option to redesign current organizational capability with an adoption decision was established. - Decision owner: not established for S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: quota windows, provider route scoring/circuit breakers, goal plan/review rounds, retry/repair feedback and global/project memory. - Closure path: no externally/future informed capability-option generation → judgment/adoption → return into current organizational capability is wired; immediate failover and goal replanning return only into current operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: role planner and goal reviewer plan/revise task actions, while router algorithms choose among configured providers; none is evidenced as autonomous prospective redesign of DaveCode&#39;s capability repertoire. - Evidence: docs/ARCHITECTURE.md; goal/orchestrator.ts; engine.ts. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: medium-high within the frozen source distribution. - Caveats: tracking provider quotas over future windows is predictive operational routing, not evidence of a distinct S4 capability-adaptation organization.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party runtime identity/ultimate-policy adjudication loop established at the declared project harness boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: goal text, route settings, allowed tools, account policy, branch/merge modes, acceptance and operator task edits constrain execution but do not decide harness ultimate identity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no identity/ultimate-policy issue is escalated, decided by legitimate authority and returned as a durable authoritative runtime policy revision. - Decision owner: not established for S5; operator/developer configuration sets constraints rather than closing a distinct VSM S5 function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: DaveConfig, DAVECODESYSTEMPROMPT, CLI/API task edits, security checks, route settings, scoped workspaces and execution limits. - Closure path: no identity/policy issue → legitimate ultimate authority → authoritative decision → returned policy governing later operation is instantiated. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: planner/reviewer/task owner decisions concern the task portfolio, not the identity of the coding organization; static config and permission gates do not exercise S5 authority by themselves. - Evidence: config/schema.ts; goal/orchestrator.ts; server/routes/tasks.ts. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high within runtime boundary. - Caveats: this negative is not a statement about maintainer governance for future DaveCode releases.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/e1e4706466182cc143e13deb1db6b560aa529b3d/assessments/da/davecode.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit e1e4706466182cc143e13deb1db6b560aa529b3d\">e1e4706 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-30T16:01:52+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/paper-instruments/feather-train\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Feather</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-30\">2026-10-30</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/paper-instruments/feather-train/tree/cdb5f34bad11c0c75ca4124b25a3e5da86bb62e2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision cdb5f34bad11c0c75ca4124b25a3e5da86bb62e2\">cdb5f34 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute user knowledge work and child assignments into deliverables. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing file contents, incomplete sources, tool errors, model-response interruptions and task-specific analysis. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select the next tool/action and decide how returned evidence changes the work or final answer. - Decision owner: parent/child model agent within its assigned task. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool schema validation, local file/shell/MCP transport, turn caps, retries, compaction and saved traces. - Closure path: model action → tool invocation → ToolMessage → next model decision → deliverable/final answer. - Boundary reachability: CLI run calls runlocal; the packaged local runner constructs FeatherAgent for parent and children. No developer-only dogfood actor is borrowed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model leaves transport and caps but no task-specific discretionary next action. - Evidence: runner, local wiring. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: source-backed availability, not an empirical claim of deliverable quality or long-run viability.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: prevent the operational team from overwriting shared deliverables or duplicating one another&#39;s work. - Distinct S1 units: parent and independently executing child assignments, including multiple concurrent children. - Inter-S1 disturbance: agents with separate histories/read revisions can edit the same shared file from incompatible prior versions; duplicated assignments can also consume effort on the same work. - Attenuating coordination relation: injected parent guidance assigns allowed files and one assembling writer for a shared workbook/document/deck; child guidance preserves others&#39; edits and prohibits writing files another agent is changing. The parent chooses these scopes and revises work through follow-ups/stop. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: statuses/reports enter the parent&#39;s next model turn; follow-up assignments specify revised work. File tools return stale-read rejection into the offending agent&#39;s next turn. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the shipped guidance specifically addresses concurrent same-file interference, chooses exclusive assembly responsibilities and prohibits overlapping writes; this is additional to generic spawn/wait. - Disturbance / variety regulated: shared-file ownership and stale views across distinct operational agents. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose who may write which deliverable and reconcile overlapping work before further changes. - Decision owner: the autonomous parent agent; children apply their scoped instructions locally. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: assignment/report tools, separate revision maps, stale-read checks. Guidance is not an atomic lock and shell writes can bypass file-tool checks. - Closure path: assignment with allowed paths → child action/report → parent reconciliation/follow-up → subsequent scoped work; stale file-tool edits return an error requiring current evidence. - Boundary reachability: runlocal appends PARENTGUIDANCE, supplies CHILDGUIDANCE through Subagents, and exposes team tools in the supported production mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model chooses the actual partition and corrective instruction; deterministic revision checking only rejects a preselected invalid write. - Evidence: coordination guidance, parent wiring, file-tool revision feedback, scripted shared-file test. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a supported behavioral regulation path, not proof that every model follows it or that simultaneous shell writes are impossible. Single-agent mode has no inter-unit coordination demand.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate current commitments of the task team and intervene when a child is unnecessary, incomplete or no longer useful to the whole deliverable. - Whole-system current view: parent context plus listagents statuses, selected waitagent reports, failed/exhausted partial-work diagnostics and compacted team-state reminders. - Current-control decision scope: decide active assignments, restrict scope/side effects, follow up on gaps, stop owned children and decide which completed work supports the overall result. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incomplete/failed children, conflicting findings and currently active commitments competing for bounded concurrency. - Decisive decision or feedback right: revise commitments and terminate a selected child after reviewing present team state; this goes beyond initial task decomposition. - Decision owner: autonomous parent model. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: configured max-concurrent/total/turn limits, status registry, cancellation and awaited resource cleanup. The host owns fixed ceilings; these are not themselves autonomous resource bargaining. - Closure path: current reports/status → model selects follow-up/stop/new work → manager changes child lifecycle → results/status feed the next parent decision. - Boundary reachability: standard production team exposes all five team tools to the parent and constructs children; local runner owns the manager lifetime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing parent discretion leaves fixed caps and automatic teardown, but no evidence-dependent choice of which commitment to revise or stop. - Evidence: model-facing team tools, status/follow-up, stop/cleanup, parent responsibility. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: one local task team&#39;s current control, not organization-wide portfolio control or authority to change host ceilings. The noninteractive CLI and general embedding API supply no distinct function-specific human-parent mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material independent complementary audit path in the assessed task-team distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: erroneous operational claims are handled by ordinary checks and parent result reconciliation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no separate audit judgment/feedback owner established. - Decision owner: ordinary producing agents and parent remain responsible for QA. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: execution traces, artifact reads and test tools provide observability. - Closure path: routine checking stays in production; no independent audit-to-control loop is supplied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a child can be asked to check work, but the generic assignment API supplies no specifically independent auditor role or complementary audit contract. - Evidence: subagent responsibility and reporting, adjacent grading, external Harbor adapter. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a host can construct a different audited system.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no shipped external-and-prospective adaptation loop at the local team boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: web/source information and model/context issues are processed as current task work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no adaptation-options-to-current-capability decision path established. - Decision owner: host selects configuration; producing model handles tasks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: optional web search/fetch, skills, compaction and separate training recipe. - Closure path: source retrieval enters task context; no distinct prospective capability-regulation conversation is supplied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: generic research or model training alone does not establish S4. - Evidence: supported runtime composition, Train&#39;s external recipe, package separation. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: future custom research assignments are not bounded absence counterexamples.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime identity/ultimate-policy resolution loop established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task scope, source trust and host constraints are applied as preselected guidance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no identity-level case → legitimate ultimate authority → returned policy path supplied. - Decision owner: host/user sets task and instructions; ordinary parent model controls current task work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: system guidance, profile validation, filesystem boundaries and configured limits. - Closure path: constraints govern tools; no distinct ultimate-policy decision-return loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: being the root task agent or accepting host instructions does not grant ultimate-policy authority. - Evidence: production guidance, CLI config/run, supported embedding limits. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: an operator&#39;s ability to replace instructions is not itself function-specific parent governance.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/48be0b607dc2399a7f268e985f4645b26679a84a/assessments/fe/feather.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 48be0b607dc2399a7f268e985f4645b26679a84a\">48be0b6 &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
